window.LD_SERIES={"title":"Before Bronze","tagline":"A documentary series in eight episodes","speakers":[{"id":"narrator","label":"Narrator","role":"Tells the story.","voice":"af_heart","lang":"en-us","color":"#eee5d4"},{"id":"research","label":"Research","role":"States what named researchers argue, in their own terms, on every side of a question.","voice":"bm_george","lang":"en-gb","color":"#e3b15c"},{"id":"source","label":"From the sources","role":"Reads historical texts: Thomsen, Lubbock, explorers, excavation diaries.","voice":"bf_emma","lang":"en-gb","color":"#c893b6"}],"audition":{"text":"In 1836 a Copenhagen museum keeper sorted the past into stone, bronze and iron. The name stuck, though stone is simply what survives.","voices":["af_heart","af_bella","af_nicole","af_sarah","am_michael","am_fenrir","bf_emma","bf_isabella","bf_alice","bf_lily","bm_george","bm_lewis","bm_daniel","bm_fable"]},"episodes":[{"id":"the-longest-age","number":1,"title":"The Longest Age","subtitle":"What the Stone Age is, why the name misleads, and how we can know anything about it","summary":"The oldest tools, the museum keeper who named the age, the clocks, molecules and ice that let us read three million years, the ice ages and the arguments about what ended them, and the whole story in one sweep.","regions":["africa","europe","asia","oceania","americas"],"emblem":"chopper","status":"draft","statusNote":"First full draft of episode 1, for feedback on voices, tone, pacing and the handling of contested views before the other episodes are written.","words":6198,"minutes":41.6,"sites":[{"id":"la-pasiega","name":"La Pasiega","country":"Spain","region":"europe","lat":43.29,"lon":-3.95,"start":64800,"end":20000,"dateLabel":"at least 64,800 years (disputed)","type":"art","summary":"A cave in Cantabria where a red ladder-shaped sign lies under a calcite crust dated by uranium-thorium to at least 64,800 years, one of three Spanish caves that Hoffmann and colleagues (2018) attributed to Neanderthal painters. The dates are contested."},{"id":"maltravieso","name":"Maltravieso","country":"Spain","region":"europe","lat":39.47,"lon":-6.39,"start":66700,"end":20000,"dateLabel":"at least 66,700 years (disputed)","type":"art","summary":"A cave in Extremadura with dozens of hand stencils; a crust over one stencil gave a minimum uranium-thorium age of about 66,700 years in the 2018 study, which critics argue may reflect uranium loss rather than true age."},{"id":"ardales","name":"Cueva de Ardales","country":"Spain","region":"europe","lat":36.87,"lon":-4.83,"start":65500,"end":20000,"dateLabel":"at least 65,500 years (disputed)","type":"art","summary":"A cave near Málaga where red pigment on stalagmite curtains was dated by crusts to at least 65,500 years; later work argued that the pigment was applied deliberately in several episodes over tens of thousands of years."},{"id":"kap-kobenhavn","name":"Kap København","country":"Greenland","region":"europe","lat":82.4,"lon":-21,"start":2000000,"end":2000000,"dateLabel":"about 2 million years ago","type":"dna","summary":"Frozen sediments at the northern tip of Greenland yielded the oldest DNA yet read, from a two-million-year-old forest with mastodons, reindeer and hares (Kjær and colleagues, 2022)."},{"id":"little-dome-c","name":"Little Dome C","country":"Antarctica","region":"oceania","lat":-75.3,"lon":122.45,"start":1200000,"end":2000,"dateLabel":"ice more than 1.2 million years old","type":"other","summary":"Drilling site of the Beyond EPICA project, where a 2,800-metre ice core reached bedrock in January 2025 with a continuous climate record of more than 1.2 million years."},{"id":"murray-springs","name":"Murray Springs","country":"United States","region":"americas","lat":31.57,"lon":-110.17,"start":13000,"end":12800,"dateLabel":"about 12,900 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"A Clovis site in Arizona where a dark organic layer, the black mat, lies over the last mammoth bones; a key site for both sides of the Younger Dryas impact debate."},{"id":"saint-acheul","name":"Saint-Acheul, Amiens","country":"France","region":"europe","lat":49.88,"lon":2.32,"start":450000,"end":300000,"dateLabel":"about 450,000–300,000 years ago","type":"tools","summary":"The gravel pits of the Somme terraces at Amiens, where handaxes lay among the bones of extinct elephants and rhinoceroses. Joseph Prestwich and John Evans watched one being dug from undisturbed gravel here in April 1859, and the Acheulean takes its name from the place."},{"id":"brixham-cave","name":"Brixham Cave (Windmill Hill Cavern)","country":"United Kingdom","region":"europe","lat":50.39,"lon":-3.51,"start":40000,"end":30000,"dateLabel":"excavated 1858–1859","type":"tools","summary":"A cave discovered by quarrying in January 1858 and excavated under William Pengelly for the Geological and Royal Societies: flint tools sealed beneath a stalagmite floor among the bones of cave bear, rhinoceros and mammoth, the first widely accepted proof that people had lived alongside extinct animals."}],"routes":[{"id":"sweep-erectus","label":"Homo erectus out of Africa","start":2100000,"end":1500000,"certainty":"debated","path":[[36,3.5],[33.5,22],[34.5,29.5],[35.56,32.69],[38.5,37],[44.34,41.34],[60,37.5],[75,39.5],[95,38],[109.49,34.22]]},{"id":"sweep-erectus-south","label":"Through southern Asia to Java","start":1800000,"end":1300000,"certainty":"debated","path":[[43.3,12.6],[52,17],[62,25.5],[72,22],[79.89,13.23],[90,23],[100,15],[104,2],[110.84,-7.46]]},{"id":"sweep-sapiens","label":"Homo sapiens across Asia to Sahul","start":70000,"end":50000,"certainty":"established","path":[[35.5,-2],[39.8,15],[43.3,12.6],[49,15],[57.5,22.5],[66.5,25],[78.5,21],[95,21.5],[103.4,20.2],[110,10],[116.2,-8.6],[124.3,-8.4],[130.2,-12.5],[132.9,-12.5]]},{"id":"sweep-europe","label":"Homo sapiens into Europe","start":48000,"end":45000,"certainty":"established","path":[[35,32],[38,35.5],[29,41],[25.4,42.9],[16.6,48.9],[11.6,50.7],[4,47]]},{"id":"sweep-americas","label":"Into the Americas","start":25000,"end":14000,"certainty":"debated","path":[[150,68],[165,67],[177,65.5],[-178,65.3],[-165,60],[-146,60],[-132,54],[-124.5,43],[-117.5,32.5],[-106.38,32.88],[-96,15.5],[-80,1],[-76.5,-14],[-73.2,-41.5]]},{"id":"sweep-pacific","label":"Lapita and Polynesian voyages","start":3350,"end":700,"certainty":"established","path":[[149.6,-1.5],[166.1,-10.2],[178,-17.8],[-175.2,-21.1],[-172,-13.8],[-149.5,-17.6],[-155.5,19.8],[-109.4,-27.1],[174,-37]]}],"scenes":[{"id":"an-edge-in-the-desert","title":"An edge in the desert","start":3300000,"end":3300000,"camera":{"lon":30,"lat":8,"zoom":1},"clock":true,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","text":"On the morning of the ninth of July 2011, a team of archaeologists took a wrong turn.","camera":{"sites":["lomekwi-3"],"zoom":4.5},"shots":[{"type":"photo","media":"turkana-sunset","move":"push","caption":"The eastern shore of Lake Turkana, Kenya"}],"sentences":["On the morning of the ninth of July 2011, a team of archaeologists took a wrong turn."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"They were looking for a fossil site west of Lake Turkana, in northern Kenya, and found instead a hillside scattered with broken stones. Sonia Harmand and Jason Lewis of Stony Brook University named the place Lomekwi 3.","sites":["lomekwi-3"],"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"lomekwi-site","move":"pan-left","caption":"Lomekwi, west of Lake Turkana"},{"at":1,"type":"globe","camera":{"sites":["lomekwi-3"],"zoom":5}}],"sentences":["They were looking for a fossil site west of Lake Turkana, in northern Kenya, and found instead a hillside scattered with broken stones.","Sonia Harmand and Jason Lewis of Stony Brook University named the place Lomekwi 3."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Someone had struck these stones from lava cobbles 3.3 million years ago. The layer that held them lies above a volcanic ash dated to 3.32 million years, and the magnetic record in the sediment agrees. If that reading holds, they are the oldest known tools made by breaking stone, 700,000 years older than any found before.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"kenyanthropus-nairobi","move":"tilt-up","caption":"Kenyanthropus platyops, found at Lomekwi in 1999, about 3.5 million years old"},{"at":1,"type":"diagram","diagram":"strata","params":{"title":"Lomekwi 3, West Turkana","every":1.5,"layers":[{"label":"Older sediments","age":"before 3.32 million years"},{"label":"Toroto Tuff","age":"volcanic ash, 3.32 million years","color":"#a99a84"},{"label":"Lomekwi 3 horizon","age":"about 3.3 million years","artefact":"excavated artefacts","at":0.5},{"label":"Younger sediments","age":"reversed magnetic field, then normal"}],"surface":"Surface finds: 130 or so pieces on the slope"}},{"at":2,"type":"number","value":"3.3 million years","label":"The oldest known knapped stone, if the context holds","kicker":"Harmand and colleagues, Nature, 2015"}],"sentences":["Someone had struck these stones from lava cobbles 3.3 million years ago.","The layer that held them lies above a volcanic ash dated to 3.32 million years, and the magnetic record in the sediment agrees.","If that reading holds, they are the oldest known tools made by breaking stone, 700,000 years older than any found before."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"From that moment until metal arrived, only 5,000 to 7,000 years ago in the places that had it first, our ancestors and their relatives lived in what we call the Stone Age. It covers more than 99 percent of the human technological past.","time":5000,"camera":{"lon":30,"lat":12,"zoom":1},"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"globe","camera":{"lon":30,"lat":12,"zoom":1}},{"at":1,"type":"number","value":"99 percent","label":"of the time since the first known tools falls within the Stone Age","kicker":"3.3 million years of stone, about 5,000 of metal"}],"sentences":["From that moment until metal arrived, only 5,000 to 7,000 years ago in the places that had it first, our ancestors and their relatives lived in what we call the Stone Age.","It covers more than 99 percent of the human technological past."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"This is the story of that age, on every continent, from the first flaked stones to the first furnaces.","shots":[{"type":"title","kicker":"Before Bronze · Episode 1","title":"The Longest Age","sub":"A world history of the Stone Age"}],"sentences":["This is the story of that age, on every continent, from the first flaked stones to the first furnaces."]}],"sources":[{"cite":"Harmand, S. et al. (2015). 3.3-million-year-old stone tools from Lomekwi 3, West Turkana, Kenya. Nature 521, 310–315.","doi":"10.1038/nature14464"}]},{"id":"a-museum-filing-system","title":"A museum’s filing system","start":5000,"end":5000,"camera":{"lon":12.58,"lat":55.68,"zoom":5},"clock":false,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","text":"The name was born in a museum. In 1816 Christian Jürgensen Thomsen, a Copenhagen merchant’s son and a self-taught antiquarian, took charge of Denmark’s collection of antiquities. He sorted its cutting tools by material, and noticed which objects turned up together in graves and hoards: first stone, then bronze, then iron. His guidebook of 1836 put the three ages into print.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"nationalmuseet","move":"push","caption":"The Prince’s Palace in Copenhagen, home of the collection Thomsen arranged"},{"at":1,"type":"photo","media":"thomsen-lithograph","move":"still","lower":{"name":"Christian Jürgensen Thomsen","sub":"Copenhagen, 1788 to 1865"}},{"at":2,"type":"montage","media":["danish-flint-dagger","danish-lur","nydam-sword"],"captions":["Stone: a flint dagger from Denmark","Bronze: lurs from Denmark","Iron: swords from the Nydam bog, Denmark"]},{"at":3,"type":"photo","media":"guide-1848-page","move":"pan-right","caption":"A page of the English edition of 1848","fit":"contain"}],"sentences":["The name was born in a museum.","In 1816 Christian Jürgensen Thomsen, a Copenhagen merchant’s son and a self-taught antiquarian, took charge of Denmark’s collection of antiquities.","He sorted its cutting tools by material, and noticed which objects turned up together in graves and hoards: first stone, then bronze, then iron.","His guidebook of 1836 put the three ages into print."]},{"speaker":"source","text":"The Age of Stone, or that period when weapons and implements were made of stone, wood, bone, or some such material, and during which very little or nothing at all was known of metals.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"type","text":"The Age of Stone, or that period when weapons and implements were made of stone, wood, bone, or some such material, and during which very little or nothing at all was known of metals.","kicker":"Guide to Northern Archaeology, London, 1848"}],"sentences":["The Age of Stone, or that period when weapons and implements were made of stone, wood, bone, or some such material, and during which very little or nothing at all was known of metals."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Thirty years later the English banker and naturalist John Lubbock split the stone part in two, in a book he called Pre-historic Times.","camera":{"lon":-0.1,"lat":51.5,"zoom":5},"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"prehistoric-times-1","move":"tilt-up","caption":"Pre-historic Times, 1865","lower":{"name":"John Lubbock","sub":"Banker, naturalist and antiquarian, 1834 to 1913"}}],"sentences":["Thirty years later the English banker and naturalist John Lubbock split the stone part in two, in a book he called Pre-historic Times."]},{"speaker":"source","text":"First, that of the Drift; when man shared the possession of Europe with the Mammoth, the Cave bear, the Woolly-haired rhinoceros, and other extinct animals. This we may call the Palaeolithic period.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"type","text":"First, that of the Drift; when man shared the possession of Europe with the Mammoth, the Cave bear, the Woolly-haired rhinoceros, and other extinct animals.","kicker":"John Lubbock, Pre-historic Times, 1865"}],"sentences":["First, that of the Drift; when man shared the possession of Europe with the Mammoth, the Cave bear, the Woolly-haired rhinoceros, and other extinct animals.","This we may call the Palaeolithic period."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Lubbock’s Palaeolithic and Neolithic, the old and the new stone ages, are still the words we use. In 1929 John Goodwin and Clarence van Riet Lowe, finding the European terms a poor fit for southern Africa, divided the African record into an Earlier, Middle and Later Stone Age instead. Every region now has its own sequence. But the label misleads. Stone is simply what survives.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"prehistoric-times-2","move":"pan-left","caption":"Pre-historic Times, 1865","fit":"contain"},{"at":1,"type":"globe","camera":{"box":[10,-36,42,-20]}},{"at":2,"type":"montage","media":["jomon-pot","clovis-point"],"captions":["Jōmon pottery, Japan: an age named after cord-marked pots","A Clovis point, North America: the Paleoindian period"]},{"at":3,"type":"type","text":"Stone is simply what survives.","kicker":"Why the name misleads"}],"sentences":["Lubbock’s Palaeolithic and Neolithic, the old and the new stone ages, are still the words we use.","In 1929 John Goodwin and Clarence van Riet Lowe, finding the European terms a poor fit for southern Africa, divided the African record into an Earlier, Middle and Later Stone Age instead.","Every region now has its own sequence.","But the label misleads.","Stone is simply what survives."]}],"sources":[{"cite":"Thomsen, C. J. (1836). Ledetraad til Nordisk Oldkyndighed. Copenhagen. English edition: Guide to Northern Archaeology, ed. the Earl of Ellesmere, London, 1848."},{"cite":"Lubbock, J. (1865). Pre-historic Times, as Illustrated by Ancient Remains, and the Manners and Customs of Modern Savages. London: Williams and Norgate."},{"cite":"Goodwin, A. J. H. & van Riet Lowe, C. (1929). The Stone Age Cultures of South Africa. Annals of the South African Museum 27, 1–289."}]},{"id":"how-deep-is-time","title":"How deep is time?","start":450000,"end":450000,"camera":{"sites":["saint-acheul"],"zoom":5},"clock":false,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Thomsen’s ages had no dates. In his day the accepted age of humanity was a few thousand years, and the idea that people had lived alongside mammoths was close to heresy. The discovery that the Stone Age was deep took two decades of argument, and it is where this series’ habit of naming who argued what begins.","shots":[{"type":"photo","media":"thomsen-museum","move":"push","caption":"Thomsen showing visitors around the museum in Copenhagen, drawing of 1848","lower":{"name":"Christian Jürgensen Thomsen","sub":"Copenhagen, 1788 to 1865"}}],"sentences":["Thomsen’s ages had no dates.","In his day the accepted age of humanity was a few thousand years, and the idea that people had lived alongside mammoths was close to heresy.","The discovery that the Stone Age was deep took two decades of argument, and it is where this series’ habit of naming who argued what begins."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"In the 1830s and 1840s Jacques Boucher de Perthes, a customs officer at Abbeville in northern France, collected flint handaxes from the gravel pits of the Somme, where they lay among the bones of extinct elephants and rhinoceroses. His book, printed in 1847, argued that people had made the tools before the biblical flood. The learned societies of Paris ignored it, and the customs officer’s belief that other flints were carved figures of animals and people did not help his case.","camera":{"lon":1.83,"lat":50.1,"zoom":5},"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"boucher-portrait","move":"pull","lower":{"name":"Jacques Boucher de Perthes","sub":"Abbeville, customs officer and antiquarian"}},{"at":1,"type":"photo","media":"boucher-biface","move":"pan-right","caption":"A flint handaxe from Menchecourt near Abbeville, from Boucher de Perthes’ collection"},{"at":2,"type":"montage","media":["moulin-quignon-bifaces","somme-river"],"captions":["Handaxes from the Moulin Quignon pit at Abbeville","The Somme valley today"]}],"sentences":["In the 1830s and 1840s Jacques Boucher de Perthes, a customs officer at Abbeville in northern France, collected flint handaxes from the gravel pits of the Somme, where they lay among the bones of extinct elephants and rhinoceroses.","His book, printed in 1847, argued that people had made the tools before the biblical flood.","The learned societies of Paris ignored it, and the customs officer’s belief that other flints were carved figures of animals and people did not help his case."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"The test came from England. In January 1858 quarrying at Brixham in Devon broke into a cave, and the Geological Society organised an excavation under the schoolteacher William Pengelly. Beneath an unbroken floor of stalagmite lay the bones of cave bear, rhinoceros and mammoth, and among them flint tools. In April 1859 the geologist Joseph Prestwich and the paper manufacturer and antiquarian John Evans crossed to Abbeville and Amiens to see the gravels for themselves. At Saint-Acheul they watched a handaxe being dug out of undisturbed gravel more than five metres down, and had it photographed in place.","sites":["brixham-cave","saint-acheul"],"camera":{"sites":["brixham-cave","saint-acheul"]},"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"brixham-entrance","move":"push","caption":"The entrance to Windmill Hill Cavern, Brixham, Devon"},{"at":1,"type":"photo","media":"pengelly-portrait","move":"still","lower":{"name":"William Pengelly","sub":"Schoolteacher and geologist, Torquay"}},{"at":2,"type":"photo","media":"brixham-section","move":"pan-right","fit":"contain","caption":"Section through Brixham Cave, from John Evans, The Ancient Stone Implements, 1872"},{"at":3,"type":"montage","media":["prestwich-portrait","evans-portrait"],"captions":["Joseph Prestwich, geologist","John Evans, paper manufacturer and antiquarian"]},{"at":4,"type":"photo","media":"saint-acheul-bifaces","move":"pull","caption":"Handaxes from the Saint-Acheul gravels, Amiens"}],"sentences":["The test came from England.","In January 1858 quarrying at Brixham in Devon broke into a cave, and the Geological Society organised an excavation under the schoolteacher William Pengelly.","Beneath an unbroken floor of stalagmite lay the bones of cave bear, rhinoceros and mammoth, and among them flint tools.","In April 1859 the geologist Joseph Prestwich and the paper manufacturer and antiquarian John Evans crossed to Abbeville and Amiens to see the gravels for themselves.","At Saint-Acheul they watched a handaxe being dug out of undisturbed gravel more than five metres down, and had it photographed in place."]},{"speaker":"source","text":"Lying in the gravel at a depth of 17 feet from the original surface, and six and a half feet from the chalk.","shots":[{"type":"type","kicker":"Joseph Prestwich, read to the Royal Society, 26 May 1859","text":"Lying in the gravel at a depth of 17 feet from the original surface, and six and a half feet from the chalk."}],"sentences":["Lying in the gravel at a depth of 17 feet from the original surface, and six and a half feet from the chalk."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Prestwich read the results to the Royal Society on the 26th of May 1859, and Evans to the Society of Antiquaries a week later. That November Darwin published On the Origin of Species, and in 1863 Charles Lyell’s Geological Evidences of the Antiquity of Man gathered the case. Within five years the human past had grown from a few thousand years to an unmeasured depth. It would take another century, and the clocks you will hear about shortly, to put numbers on it.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"diagram","diagram":"years","params":{"title":"Five years that deepened the past","from":1857,"to":1864,"tick":1,"every":2.2,"events":[{"year":1858,"label":"Brixham Cave|excavated"},{"year":1859.3,"label":"Prestwich and Evans|at Abbeville"},{"year":1859.9,"label":"Darwin,|On the Origin of Species"},{"year":1863,"label":"Lyell, The Antiquity|of Man"}]}},{"at":2,"type":"montage","media":["darwin-1859","origin-title","lyell-portrait"],"captions":["Charles Darwin, photographed in 1855","On the Origin of Species, first edition, 1859","Charles Lyell, about 1863"]}],"sentences":["Prestwich read the results to the Royal Society on the 26th of May 1859, and Evans to the Society of Antiquaries a week later.","That November Darwin published On the Origin of Species, and in 1863 Charles Lyell’s Geological Evidences of the Antiquity of Man gathered the case.","Within five years the human past had grown from a few thousand years to an unmeasured depth.","It would take another century, and the clocks you will hear about shortly, to put numbers on it."]}],"sources":[{"cite":"Boucher de Perthes, J. (1847, published 1849). Antiquités celtiques et antédiluviennes. Paris: Treuttel et Wurtz."},{"cite":"Prestwich, J. (1860). On the occurrence of flint-implements, associated with the remains of animals of extinct species in beds of a late geological period, in France at Amiens and Abbeville, and in England at Hoxne. Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society 150, 277–317."},{"cite":"Evans, J. (1860). On the occurrence of flint implements in undisturbed beds of gravel, sand, and clay. Archaeologia 38, 280–307."},{"cite":"Gamble, C. & Kruszynski, R. (2009). John Evans, Joseph Prestwich and the stone that shattered the time barrier. Antiquity 83, 461–475."},{"cite":"Lyell, C. (1863). The Geological Evidences of the Antiquity of Man. London: John Murray."}]},{"id":"an-argument-at-the-beginning","title":"An argument at the beginning","start":3300000,"end":2600000,"camera":{"sites":["lomekwi-3","nyayanga","ledi-geraru","gona"],"zoom":3.4},"clock":true,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Even the beginning of the age is argued over. Lomekwi 3 is a single site, and a claim this old draws hard questions. Throughout this series, each side will be stated as its supporters put it.","sites":["lomekwi-3"],"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"lomekwi-site","move":"pull","caption":"Lomekwi, west of Lake Turkana"},{"at":1,"type":"globe","camera":{"sites":["lomekwi-3"],"zoom":5}},{"at":2,"type":"type","text":"Each side, as its supporters put it.","kicker":"The rule of this series"}],"sentences":["Even the beginning of the age is argued over.","Lomekwi 3 is a single site, and a claim this old draws hard questions.","Throughout this series, each side will be stated as its supporters put it."]},{"speaker":"research","text":"Sonia Harmand, Jason Lewis and their colleagues, writing in Nature in 2015, describe 149 artefacts, about twenty of them excavated from undisturbed sediment. The cores and flakes carry the scars that repeated, deliberate striking produces, and several flakes fit back onto their cores.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"oldowan-chopper","move":"push","caption":"The marks of knapping on an early core (an Oldowan example)"},{"at":1,"type":"photo","media":"refitting","move":"pan-right","caption":"Refitting: flakes fitted back onto the core they were struck from"}],"sentences":["Sonia Harmand, Jason Lewis and their colleagues, writing in Nature in 2015, describe 149 artefacts, about twenty of them excavated from undisturbed sediment.","The cores and flakes carry the scars that repeated, deliberate striking produces, and several flakes fit back onto their cores."]},{"speaker":"research","text":"Manuel Domínguez-Rodrigo and Luis Alcalá, in 2016 and again in 2019, answer that most of the pieces lay on the surface, where they could have arrived from younger layers, and that natural processes can break stone in ways that imitate knapping. In 2020 Will Archer, Vera Aldeias and Shannon McPherron argued that the original paper had stretched the meaning of the words in situ. Harmand and her colleagues replied that the excavated pieces came from an intact layer, and that the surface finds share the same raw materials and the same style.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"split","question":"Are the Lomekwi 3 stones the oldest tools?","views":[{"label":"Yes, 3.3 million years","text":"Harmand, Lewis and colleagues, 2015: artefacts excavated in place, knapping scars and refits, a layer above an ash dated to 3.32 million years, and a matching magnetic record.","media":"lomekwi-site"},{"label":"Not yet shown","text":"Domínguez-Rodrigo and Alcalá, 2016 and 2019: most pieces were surface finds, the excavated sample is small, and natural breakage can imitate knapping. Archer, Aldeias and McPherron, 2020: the paper stretched the meaning of in situ.","media":"kenyanthropus-nairobi"}],"status":"Widely cited as the oldest evidence of knapping, but still one site. A second site of similar age, or new excavation at Lomekwi, would settle it.","stagger":9}],"sentences":["Manuel Domínguez-Rodrigo and Luis Alcalá, in 2016 and again in 2019, answer that most of the pieces lay on the surface, where they could have arrived from younger layers, and that natural processes can break stone in ways that imitate knapping.","In 2020 Will Archer, Vera Aldeias and Shannon McPherron argued that the original paper had stretched the meaning of the words in situ.","Harmand and her colleagues replied that the excavated pieces came from an intact layer, and that the surface finds share the same raw materials and the same style."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Where does the field stand? The 3.3-million-year date is widely cited, and no second site that old has been found. The continuous record of flaked tools begins between 3 and 2.6 million years ago, at Nyayanga in Kenya and at Ledi-Geraru and Gona in Ethiopia.","sites":["nyayanga","ledi-geraru","gona"],"time":2600000,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"globe","camera":{"sites":["lomekwi-3","nyayanga","ledi-geraru","gona"],"zoom":3.4}},{"at":2,"type":"montage","media":["nyayanga-site","gona-tools"],"captions":["Nyayanga, Kenya: Oldowan tools of 3 to 2.6 million years","Oldowan tools from Gona, Ethiopia"]}],"sentences":["Where does the field stand?","The 3.3-million-year date is widely cited, and no second site that old has been found.","The continuous record of flaked tools begins between 3 and 2.6 million years ago, at Nyayanga in Kenya and at Ledi-Geraru and Gona in Ethiopia."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Either way, the Stone Age did not begin with one invention, but with a slow change in how our relatives used stone. We will return to Lomekwi in the next episode. First, a question this whole series depends on: how can we know any of this?","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"turkana-shore","move":"push","caption":"The southern shore of Lake Turkana"},{"at":2,"type":"type","text":"How can we know any of this?","kicker":"Coming up"}],"sentences":["Either way, the Stone Age did not begin with one invention, but with a slow change in how our relatives used stone.","We will return to Lomekwi in the next episode.","First, a question this whole series depends on: how can we know any of this?"]}],"sources":[{"cite":"Harmand, S. et al. (2015). 3.3-million-year-old stone tools from Lomekwi 3, West Turkana, Kenya. Nature 521, 310–315.","doi":"10.1038/nature14464"},{"cite":"Domínguez-Rodrigo, M. & Alcalá, L. (2016). 3.3-Million-Year-Old Stone Tools and Butchery Traces? More Evidence Needed. PaleoAnthropology 2016, 46–53."},{"cite":"Domínguez-Rodrigo, M. & Alcalá, L. (2019). Pliocene Archaeology at Lomekwi 3? New Evidence Fuels More Skepticism. Journal of African Archaeology 17, 173–176."},{"cite":"Harmand, S. et al. (2019). Reply to Domínguez-Rodrigo and Alcalá: Interpretation without accurate evidence is fantasy. Journal of African Archaeology 17, 177–181."},{"cite":"Archer, W., Aldeias, V. & McPherron, S. P. (2020). What is ‘in situ’? A reply to Harmand et al. (2015). Journal of Human Evolution 142, 102740.","doi":"10.1016/j.jhevol.2020.102740"},{"cite":"Plummer, T. W. et al. (2023). Expanded geographic distribution and dietary strategies of the earliest Oldowan hominins and Paranthropus. Science 379, 561–566.","doi":"10.1126/science.abo7452"},{"cite":"Williams, E. M., Key, A., de la Torre, I. & Wood, B. (2025). Who made the Oldowan? Reviewing African hominin fossils and archaeological sites from 3.5 million years ago. Journal of Anthropological Archaeology 79, 101704.","doi":"10.1016/j.jaa.2025.101704"}]},{"id":"more-than-stones","title":"More than stones","start":476000,"end":37000,"camera":{"sites":["schoningen"],"zoom":4.5},"clock":true,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Stone dominates museum cases for one reason: it survives. Almost everything else rots. Where conditions are exceptional, glimpses of the rest appear.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"saint-acheul-bifaces","move":"pull","caption":"What survives: flint from Saint-Acheul"},{"at":2,"type":"photo","media":"schoningen-spear","move":"pan-right","caption":"A wooden spear from Schöningen, Germany"}],"sentences":["Stone dominates museum cases for one reason: it survives.","Almost everything else rots.","Where conditions are exceptional, glimpses of the rest appear."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"In an open-cast coal mine at Schöningen, in northern Germany, excavators in the 1990s found wooden spears up to two and a half metres long, lying among the bones of some fifty butchered horses. Lakeshore mud had sealed them from oxygen. For years the spears were dated to about 300,000 years. In 2025 a team led by Jarod Hutson, with dating by Kirsty Penkman’s laboratory in York, measured the slow chemical change of amino acids sealed inside snail shells from the same layer and put the horizon at about 200,000 years, which would make the hunters Neanderthals rather than their ancestors.","sites":["schoningen"],"time":200000,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"schoningen-museum","move":"push","caption":"The Schöningen research museum, beside the open-cast mine"},{"at":1,"type":"photo","media":"schoningen-spear","move":"tilt-up","caption":"The spears were sealed in lakeshore mud"},{"at":2,"type":"number","value":"300,000 years","label":"The spears’ age, as published until 2025","kicker":"Schöningen"},{"at":3,"type":"number","value":"200,000 years","label":"The 2025 amino acid age, which would make the hunters Neanderthals","kicker":"Hutson and colleagues, Science Advances, 2025"}],"sentences":["In an open-cast coal mine at Schöningen, in northern Germany, excavators in the 1990s found wooden spears up to two and a half metres long, lying among the bones of some fifty butchered horses.","Lakeshore mud had sealed them from oxygen.","For years the spears were dated to about 300,000 years.","In 2025 a team led by Jarod Hutson, with dating by Kirsty Penkman’s laboratory in York, measured the slow chemical change of amino acids sealed inside snail shells from the same layer and put the horizon at about 200,000 years, which would make the hunters Neanderthals rather than their ancestors."]},{"speaker":"research","text":"The two dates rest on different evidence. The older figure came from the sequence of glacial deposits above and below the finds; the newer one from amino acids, whose rate of change depends on the temperatures the shells experienced over thousands of years. The two lines of evidence do not yet agree, and both dates appear in the literature.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"split","question":"How old are the Schöningen spears?","views":[{"label":"About 300,000 years","text":"The sequence of glacial deposits above and below the finds, matched to the marine isotope stages."},{"label":"About 200,000 years","text":"Hutson and colleagues, 2025: amino acids sealed in snail shells from the same layer, whose rate of change depends on the temperatures they experienced."}],"status":"Both dates appear in the literature; the two lines of evidence do not yet agree.","stagger":5}],"sentences":["The two dates rest on different evidence.","The older figure came from the sequence of glacial deposits above and below the finds; the newer one from amino acids, whose rate of change depends on the temperatures the shells experienced over thousands of years.","The two lines of evidence do not yet agree, and both dates appear in the literature."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"String is another example. In 2020 Bruce Hardy and colleagues reported a fragment of three-ply cord, twisted from the inner bark of a conifer, stuck to a stone tool at Abri du Maras in southern France and made by Neanderthals roughly 50,000 years ago. In 2024 Nicholas Conard and Veerle Rots showed that a baton of mammoth ivory from Hohle Fels in southwest Germany, about 37,000 years old and pierced by four spirally grooved holes, was most likely a tool for making rope. With replicas, experimenters twisted cattail fibres into metres of strong cord in minutes.","sites":["abri-du-maras","hohle-fels"],"camera":{"sites":["abri-du-maras","hohle-fels"]},"time":40000,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"cattail","move":"push","caption":"Cattail: the fibre twisted into rope in the Hohle Fels experiments"},{"at":1,"type":"photo","media":"abri-du-maras-photo","move":"pan-left","caption":"Abri du Maras, Ardèche, where a Neanderthal cord survived on a stone tool"},{"at":2,"type":"photo","media":"hohle-fels-cave","move":"tilt-up","caption":"Hohle Fels in the Swabian Jura, where the ivory rope-making tool was found"}],"sentences":["String is another example.","In 2020 Bruce Hardy and colleagues reported a fragment of three-ply cord, twisted from the inner bark of a conifer, stuck to a stone tool at Abri du Maras in southern France and made by Neanderthals roughly 50,000 years ago.","In 2024 Nicholas Conard and Veerle Rots showed that a baton of mammoth ivory from Hohle Fels in southwest Germany, about 37,000 years old and pierced by four spirally grooved holes, was most likely a tool for making rope.","With replicas, experimenters twisted cattail fibres into metres of strong cord in minutes."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"At Kalambo Falls in Zambia, two logs notched to fit together, at least 476,000 years old, were preserved by waterlogging and published in 2023 by Larry Barham and colleagues. Every such find is a reminder that the Stone Age toolkit was mostly not stone, and that the record we have is a record of what lasts.","sites":["kalambo-falls"],"camera":{"sites":["kalambo-falls"],"zoom":3.5},"time":476000,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"kalambo-falls-photo","move":"push","caption":"Kalambo Falls, Zambia"},{"at":1,"type":"type","text":"The record we have is a record of what lasts.","kicker":"More than stones"}],"sentences":["At Kalambo Falls in Zambia, two logs notched to fit together, at least 476,000 years old, were preserved by waterlogging and published in 2023 by Larry Barham and colleagues.","Every such find is a reminder that the Stone Age toolkit was mostly not stone, and that the record we have is a record of what lasts."]}],"sources":[{"cite":"Hutson, J. M. et al. (2025). Revised age for Schöningen hunting spears indicates intensification of Neanderthal cooperative behavior around 200,000 years ago. Science Advances 11, eadv0752.","doi":"10.1126/sciadv.adv0752"},{"cite":"Hardy, B. L. et al. (2020). Direct evidence of Neanderthal fibre technology and its cognitive and behavioral implications. Scientific Reports 10, 4889.","doi":"10.1038/s41598-020-61839-w"},{"cite":"Conard, N. J. & Rots, V. (2024). Rope making in the Aurignacian of Central Europe more than 35,000 years ago. Science Advances 10, eadh5217.","doi":"10.1126/sciadv.adh5217"},{"cite":"Barham, L. et al. (2023). Evidence for the earliest structural use of wood at least 476,000 years ago. Nature 622, 107–111.","doi":"10.1038/s41586-023-06557-9"}]},{"id":"reading-the-ground","title":"Reading the ground","start":3390000,"end":3390000,"camera":{"sites":["dikika"],"zoom":4.5},"clock":true,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Before the clocks, a word about ground. Archaeology reads layers. Sediment settles in order, oldest at the bottom, and an object sealed in an undisturbed layer belongs to the time of that layer. Most arguments about early sites, Lomekwi included, are arguments about whether a layer really was undisturbed, and whether the objects in it are what they seem.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"gran-dolina","move":"tilt-up","caption":"Excavation at Gran Dolina, Atapuerca, Spain, 2008: a million years of layers"},{"at":2,"type":"diagram","diagram":"strata","params":{"title":"Reading layers","every":1.3,"layers":[{"label":"Bedrock","age":"the floor of the record"},{"label":"River gravels","age":"older, laid down first"},{"label":"Fine silt","age":"with an object sealed in place","artefact":"excavated in place","at":0.6},{"label":"Later sediments","age":"younger, laid down last"},{"label":"Topsoil","age":"today"}],"surface":"Surface finds: anything from any layer above, washed down"}}],"sentences":["Before the clocks, a word about ground.","Archaeology reads layers.","Sediment settles in order, oldest at the bottom, and an object sealed in an undisturbed layer belongs to the time of that layer.","Most arguments about early sites, Lomekwi included, are arguments about whether a layer really was undisturbed, and whether the objects in it are what they seem."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Two bones from Dikika in Ethiopia show how hard the second question can be. In 2010 Shannon McPherron, Zeresenay Alemseged and colleagues reported marks on two animal bones 3.39 million years old that they read as cuts and scrapes from stone tools, which would put butchery 800,000 years before the earliest known tools.","sites":["dikika"],"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"selam-collections","move":"pan-right","caption":"Selam, the Dikika child, about 3.3 million years old, National Museum of Ethiopia"},{"at":1,"type":"photo","media":"alemseged","move":"still","lower":{"name":"Zeresenay Alemseged","sub":"Dikika Research Project"}}],"sentences":["Two bones from Dikika in Ethiopia show how hard the second question can be.","In 2010 Shannon McPherron, Zeresenay Alemseged and colleagues reported marks on two animal bones 3.39 million years old that they read as cuts and scrapes from stone tools, which would put butchery 800,000 years before the earliest known tools."]},{"speaker":"research","text":"Manuel Domínguez-Rodrigo, Travis Pickering and Henry Bunn answered the same year that the marks matched damage from bones trampled in abrasive sand, and that no tools had been found. McPherron’s team replied that the shape, position and internal features of the marks matched experimental cuts rather than trampling. Statistical methods that separate cut marks from trampling have improved since, but two bones are a small sample, and the question is still open.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"split","question":"Were the Dikika bones butchered 3.39 million years ago?","stagger":6,"views":[{"label":"Cut marks","text":"McPherron, Alemseged and colleagues, 2010: the shape, position and internal features of the marks match stone-tool cuts and percussion, not trampling.","media":"selam-collections"},{"label":"Trampling","text":"Domínguez-Rodrigo, Pickering and Bunn, 2010: the marks match experimental damage from bones trampled in abrasive sand, and no tools were found.","media":"afar-sites-map"}],"status":"Still open. Methods that separate cut marks from trampling statistically have improved, but two bones are a small sample."}],"sentences":["Manuel Domínguez-Rodrigo, Travis Pickering and Henry Bunn answered the same year that the marks matched damage from bones trampled in abrasive sand, and that no tools had been found.","McPherron’s team replied that the shape, position and internal features of the marks matched experimental cuts rather than trampling.","Statistical methods that separate cut marks from trampling have improved since, but two bones are a small sample, and the question is still open."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"The discipline that studies what happens to bones and stones after they are dropped, from trampling and gnawing to river transport and the collapse of cave floors, is called taphonomy, and it will sit behind many of the arguments in this series: whether the dead of Homo naledi were buried or washed in; whether a site in California holds mastodon bones broken by people 130,000 years ago; whether the sand at Madjedbebe kept its artefacts in place for 65,000 years.","camera":{"lon":40,"lat":0,"zoom":1},"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"olduvai-panorama","move":"pan-left","caption":"Olduvai Gorge, Tanzania, where rivers, lakes and volcanic ash sorted bones and stones for two million years"}],"sentences":["The discipline that studies what happens to bones and stones after they are dropped, from trampling and gnawing to river transport and the collapse of cave floors, is called taphonomy, and it will sit behind many of the arguments in this series: whether the dead of Homo naledi were buried or washed in; whether a site in California holds mastodon bones broken by people 130,000 years ago; whether the sand at Madjedbebe kept its artefacts in place for 65,000 years."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Context is why archaeologists dig slowly, record every find in three dimensions, and are wary of objects picked up from the surface. When you hear that a find was excavated in place, or that it was a surface find, you now know why the words matter.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"section-drawing","move":"push","fit":"contain","caption":"A section drawing: the record of what lay above what"},{"at":1,"type":"globe","camera":{"sites":["dikika"],"zoom":4}}],"sentences":["Context is why archaeologists dig slowly, record every find in three dimensions, and are wary of objects picked up from the surface.","When you hear that a find was excavated in place, or that it was a surface find, you now know why the words matter."]}],"sources":[{"cite":"McPherron, S. P. et al. (2010). Evidence for stone-tool-assisted consumption of animal tissues before 3.39 million years ago at Dikika, Ethiopia. Nature 466, 857–860.","doi":"10.1038/nature09248"},{"cite":"Domínguez-Rodrigo, M., Pickering, T. R. & Bunn, H. T. (2010). Configurational approach to identifying the earliest hominin butchers. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 107, 20929–20934."},{"cite":"Lyman, R. L. (1994). Vertebrate Taphonomy. Cambridge University Press."}]},{"id":"clocks-in-rocks","title":"Clocks in rocks","start":50000,"end":50000,"camera":{"lon":20,"lat":10,"zoom":1},"clock":true,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","text":"How can anyone know that a spear is 200,000 years old, or a stone tool 3.3 million? Most of the dates in this series come from physics: from clocks that started ticking when an ash fell, a grain of sand was buried, or a plant died.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"tree-rings","move":"push","caption":"Tree rings: the calendar against which radiocarbon is calibrated"},{"at":1,"type":"montage","media":["mass-spectrometer","stalagmite-cave","sterkfontein-caves"],"captions":["Clocks in the laboratory","Clocks in cave calcite","Clocks in buried quartz"]}],"sentences":["How can anyone know that a spear is 200,000 years old, or a stone tool 3.3 million?","Most of the dates in this series come from physics: from clocks that started ticking when an ash fell, a grain of sand was buried, or a plant died."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"The most familiar clock is radiocarbon. Cosmic rays make a radioactive form of carbon in the upper air, and every living thing takes it up. When it dies, the intake stops and the carbon-14 decays, halving every 5,730 years. Willard Libby published the first dates in 1949 and received the Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 1960. After about 50,000 years too little carbon-14 remains to measure, so radiocarbon covers only the last hundredth of the story. Raw ages also have to be calibrated against tree rings, cave deposits and lake sediments, because the amount of carbon-14 in the air has varied; the international calibration curve was last revised in 2020 and reaches back 55,000 years.","time":50000,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"libby","move":"still","lower":{"name":"Willard Libby","sub":"Nobel Prize in Chemistry, 1960"}},{"at":1,"type":"diagram","diagram":"decay","params":{"title":"Radiocarbon: carbon-14 halves every 5,730 years","halfLife":5730,"halves":9,"secPerHalf":1.7,"limit":"After about 50,000 years too little is left to measure"}},{"at":5,"type":"photo","media":"ams-lab","move":"pan-right","caption":"An accelerator mass spectrometer counts carbon-14 atoms directly"}],"sentences":["The most familiar clock is radiocarbon.","Cosmic rays make a radioactive form of carbon in the upper air, and every living thing takes it up.","When it dies, the intake stops and the carbon-14 decays, halving every 5,730 years.","Willard Libby published the first dates in 1949 and received the Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 1960.","After about 50,000 years too little carbon-14 remains to measure, so radiocarbon covers only the last hundredth of the story.","Raw ages also have to be calibrated against tree rings, cave deposits and lake sediments, because the amount of carbon-14 in the air has varied; the international calibration curve was last revised in 2020 and reaches back 55,000 years."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Older sites need older clocks. Volcanic ash and lava contain potassium, which decays into argon gas at a known rate; the heat of an eruption drives out the old argon and resets the clock. In 1961 Jack Evernden and Garniss Curtis dated the ash beneath the skull that Mary Leakey had found at Olduvai Gorge in Tanzania to 1.75 million years, far older than almost anyone expected, and the human story suddenly grew. East Africa’s Rift Valley is layered with ash, which is why it provides the backbone of dates for early human fossils.","sites":["olduvai-gorge"],"camera":{"sites":["olduvai-gorge"],"zoom":4},"time":1750000,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"olduvai-panorama","move":"pan-left","caption":"Olduvai Gorge, Tanzania"},{"at":1,"type":"photo","media":"curtis","move":"still","lower":{"name":"Garniss Curtis","sub":"Potassium-argon dating, Berkeley"}},{"at":2,"type":"photo","media":"zinj-skull","move":"push","caption":"OH 5, the skull Mary Leakey found at Olduvai in 1959","lower":{"name":"Mary Leakey","sub":"Olduvai Gorge, 1959"}},{"at":3,"type":"photo","media":"olduvai-space","move":"tilt-up","caption":"Olduvai Gorge from space: layers of ash and lake sediment"}],"sentences":["Older sites need older clocks.","Volcanic ash and lava contain potassium, which decays into argon gas at a known rate; the heat of an eruption drives out the old argon and resets the clock.","In 1961 Jack Evernden and Garniss Curtis dated the ash beneath the skull that Mary Leakey had found at Olduvai Gorge in Tanzania to 1.75 million years, far older than almost anyone expected, and the human story suddenly grew.","East Africa’s Rift Valley is layered with ash, which is why it provides the backbone of dates for early human fossils."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Earth’s magnetic field has flipped many times. Magnetic minerals in cooling lava or settling mud record the direction of the field, and the last full reversal, about 773,000 years ago, is a marker recognisable in sediments worldwide. A layer magnetised the wrong way round must be older than that.","time":773000,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"diagram","diagram":"reversals","params":{"secs":9,"maxMa":3.5}}],"sentences":["Earth’s magnetic field has flipped many times.","Magnetic minerals in cooling lava or settling mud record the direction of the field, and the last full reversal, about 773,000 years ago, is a marker recognisable in sediments worldwide.","A layer magnetised the wrong way round must be older than that."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Other clocks count damage. Natural radiation traps electrons in the crystals of quartz and feldspar; sunlight or heat empties the traps. Luminescence dating measures how much has built up since a grain of sand last saw daylight, or a flint was last burnt. Electron spin resonance does the same for tooth enamel. Uranium dissolved in cave water is built into stalagmites and crusts, and its decay into thorium dates them back to about half a million years. And cosmic rays make rare isotopes in quartz at the surface, which decay at different rates once a rock is washed deep into a cave: that is how the skeleton called Little Foot at Sterkfontein in South Africa was dated to about 3.67 million years by Darryl Granger and colleagues in 2015.","sites":["sterkfontein"],"camera":{"sites":["sterkfontein"],"zoom":4},"time":3670000,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"stalagmite-cave","move":"tilt-up","caption":"Stalagmites and crusts: clocks of uranium and thorium"},{"at":4,"type":"photo","media":"sterkfontein-caves","move":"push","caption":"The Sterkfontein caves, South Africa"},{"at":5,"type":"photo","media":"little-foot","move":"pan-right","caption":"Little Foot, the most complete australopith skeleton, Sterkfontein"}],"sentences":["Other clocks count damage.","Natural radiation traps electrons in the crystals of quartz and feldspar; sunlight or heat empties the traps.","Luminescence dating measures how much has built up since a grain of sand last saw daylight, or a flint was last burnt.","Electron spin resonance does the same for tooth enamel.","Uranium dissolved in cave water is built into stalagmites and crusts, and its decay into thorium dates them back to about half a million years.","And cosmic rays make rare isotopes in quartz at the surface, which decay at different rates once a rock is washed deep into a cave: that is how the skeleton called Little Foot at Sterkfontein in South Africa was dated to about 3.67 million years by Darryl Granger and colleagues in 2015."]},{"speaker":"research","text":"The Little Foot date shows how dating arguments run. Jan Kramers and Paul Dirks argued in 2017 that the quartz grains could have been washed in long before the skeleton, and that flowstones in the cave point to an age nearer 2.2 million years. Granger’s team answered that the flowstones had formed in later cavities and could not date the bones. In 2024 Francis Thackeray compared the fossil primates of the deposit with dated sequences elsewhere and arrived at about 3.6 million years, though earlier comparisons of the same kind had suggested between 2 and 2.6 million. Most researchers now accept an age near 3.7 million, with the caveat that a single site dated by one main method invites doubt.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"split","question":"How old is Little Foot?","views":[{"label":"About 3.67 million years","text":"Granger and colleagues, 2015 and 2022: cosmogenic isotopes in quartz grains buried with the skeleton, on an isochron that corrects for reworked grains.","media":"little-foot"},{"label":"Nearer 2.2 million years","text":"Kramers and Dirks, 2017: the grains may predate the skeleton, and flowstones in the chamber give younger ages.","media":"sterkfontein-caves"}],"status":"Thackeray, 2024, reached about 3.6 million years from the fossil primates; the older age is the majority view, but it rests mainly on one method at one site.","stagger":8}],"sentences":["The Little Foot date shows how dating arguments run.","Jan Kramers and Paul Dirks argued in 2017 that the quartz grains could have been washed in long before the skeleton, and that flowstones in the cave point to an age nearer 2.2 million years.","Granger’s team answered that the flowstones had formed in later cavities and could not date the bones.","In 2024 Francis Thackeray compared the fossil primates of the deposit with dated sequences elsewhere and arrived at about 3.6 million years, though earlier comparisons of the same kind had suggested between 2 and 2.6 million.","Most researchers now accept an age near 3.7 million, with the caveat that a single site dated by one main method invites doubt."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"No method is perfect, so a well-dated site is dated by several, and this series gives dates as ranges, naming the method where it matters.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"type","text":"No method is perfect, so a well-dated site is dated by several.","kicker":"The rule for dates"}],"sentences":["No method is perfect, so a well-dated site is dated by several, and this series gives dates as ranges, naming the method where it matters."]}],"sources":[{"cite":"Libby, W. F., Anderson, E. C. & Arnold, J. R. (1949). Age determination by radiocarbon content: world-wide assay of natural radiocarbon. Science 109, 227–228."},{"cite":"Reimer, P. J. et al. (2020). The IntCal20 Northern Hemisphere radiocarbon age calibration curve (0–55 cal kBP). Radiocarbon 62, 725–757.","doi":"10.1017/RDC.2020.41"},{"cite":"Leakey, L. S. B., Evernden, J. F. & Curtis, G. H. (1961). Age of Bed I, Olduvai Gorge, Tanganyika. Nature 191, 478–479."},{"cite":"Granger, D. E. et al. (2015). New cosmogenic burial ages for Sterkfontein Member 2 Australopithecus and Member 5 Oldowan. Nature 522, 85–88.","doi":"10.1038/nature14268"},{"cite":"Kramers, J. D. & Dirks, P. H. G. M. (2017). The age of fossil StW573 (‘Little Foot’): an alternative interpretation of 26Al/10Be burial data. South African Journal of Science 113."},{"cite":"Thackeray, J. F. (2024). A biochronological date of 3.6 million years for “Little Foot” (StW 573, Australopithecus prometheus from Sterkfontein, South Africa). Evolutionary Anthropology 33, e22049.","doi":"10.1002/evan.22049"}]},{"id":"a-date-in-dispute","title":"A date in dispute","start":65000,"end":51000,"camera":{"sites":["la-pasiega","maltravieso","ardales"],"zoom":5},"clock":true,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Sometimes a single date can move a whole debate. In 2018 Dirk Hoffmann, Alistair Pike and colleagues reported uranium-thorium dates on thin calcite crusts that had grown over red paintings in three Spanish caves: La Pasiega, Maltravieso and Ardales. The crusts were at least 64,800 years old. Modern humans reached Spain more than 20,000 years later, so if the dates hold, the painters were Neanderthals.","sites":["la-pasiega","maltravieso","ardales"],"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"la-pasiega","move":"push","caption":"La Pasiega, Cantabria"},{"at":1,"type":"photo","media":"maltravieso","move":"pan-right","caption":"Hand stencils at Maltravieso, Extremadura"},{"at":2,"type":"number","value":"64,800 years","label":"Minimum age of the calcite over the paint at La Pasiega","kicker":"Hoffmann and colleagues, Science, 2018"},{"at":3,"type":"photo","media":"ardales","move":"tilt-up","caption":"Cueva de Ardales, Málaga"}],"sentences":["Sometimes a single date can move a whole debate.","In 2018 Dirk Hoffmann, Alistair Pike and colleagues reported uranium-thorium dates on thin calcite crusts that had grown over red paintings in three Spanish caves: La Pasiega, Maltravieso and Ardales.","The crusts were at least 64,800 years old.","Modern humans reached Spain more than 20,000 years later, so if the dates hold, the painters were Neanderthals."]},{"speaker":"research","text":"The team argued that calcite can only grow after the paint beneath it is in place, so its age is a minimum age for the art, and that they had sampled many crusts, at three caves, with consistent results.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"split","question":"Did Neanderthals paint Spanish caves 65,000 years ago?","views":[{"label":"Yes: the crusts are minimum ages","text":"Hoffmann, Pike and colleagues, 2018: uranium-thorium dates on calcite over the paint at three caves, consistent and older than any modern human presence in Spain.","media":"la-pasiega"},{"label":"The dates may be too old","text":"White and 43 colleagues, 2020; Pons-Branchu and colleagues, 2020: flowing water leaches uranium and inflates ages; some samples were beside the pigment; no other Neanderthal painting is known.","media":"nerja-cave"}],"status":"Published and contested. No independent method has confirmed or refuted the dates, and the authors stand by them.","stagger":7}],"sentences":["The team argued that calcite can only grow after the paint beneath it is in place, so its age is a minimum age for the art, and that they had sampled many crusts, at three caves, with consistent results."]},{"speaker":"research","text":"In 2020 a group of 44 researchers led by Randall White answered that water flowing over cave walls can leach uranium out of calcite, making it appear older than it is, that some crusts sampled lay beside rather than over the pigment, and that no other Neanderthal site in Europe shows painting. Edwige Pons-Branchu and colleagues showed at Nerja Cave, also in Spain, that the same method can give ages far too old for layers of known age. Hoffmann and Pike replied that they had tested for uranium loss and found none where it mattered, and stood by their dates.","sentences":["In 2020 a group of 44 researchers led by Randall White answered that water flowing over cave walls can leach uranium out of calcite, making it appear older than it is, that some crusts sampled lay beside rather than over the pigment, and that no other Neanderthal site in Europe shows painting.","Edwige Pons-Branchu and colleagues showed at Nerja Cave, also in Spain, that the same method can give ages far too old for layers of known age.","Hoffmann and Pike replied that they had tested for uranium loss and found none where it mattered, and stood by their dates."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Where does it stand? The dates remain published and contested, and no independent method has yet confirmed or refuted them. Meanwhile uranium-series dating of a hand stencil on Muna Island and a painted hunting scene in Sulawesi, both in Indonesia, gave ages of at least 67,800 and 51,200 years, with the same method and similar questions about calcite. We will return to all of these caves. The point for now is that a date is an argument until other methods and other sites agree.","sites":["liang-metanduno","maros-pangkep-karst"],"camera":{"sites":["liang-metanduno","maros-pangkep-karst"],"zoom":4},"time":51200,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"globe","camera":{"sites":["la-pasiega","maltravieso","ardales"],"zoom":5}},{"at":2,"type":"photo","media":"sulawesi-hand-stencil","move":"push","caption":"Hand stencils in Sulawesi, Indonesia, dated by the same method"},{"at":4,"type":"type","text":"A date is an argument until other methods and other sites agree.","kicker":"Dating"}],"sentences":["Where does it stand?","The dates remain published and contested, and no independent method has yet confirmed or refuted them.","Meanwhile uranium-series dating of a hand stencil on Muna Island and a painted hunting scene in Sulawesi, both in Indonesia, gave ages of at least 67,800 and 51,200 years, with the same method and similar questions about calcite.","We will return to all of these caves.","The point for now is that a date is an argument until other methods and other sites agree."]}],"sources":[{"cite":"Hoffmann, D. L. et al. (2018). U-Th dating of carbonate crusts reveals Neandertal origin of Iberian cave art. Science 359, 912–915.","doi":"10.1126/science.aap7778"},{"cite":"White, R. et al. (2020). Still no archaeological evidence that Neanderthals created Iberian cave art. Journal of Human Evolution 144, 102640.","doi":"10.1016/j.jhevol.2019.102640"},{"cite":"Pons-Branchu, E. et al. (2020). U-series dating at Nerja cave reveal open system. Implications for the Neanderthal origin of cave art. Journal of Archaeological Science 117, 105120."},{"cite":"Hoffmann, D. L. et al. (2020). Response to White et al.’s reply: ‘Still no archaeological evidence that Neanderthals created Iberian cave art’. Journal of Human Evolution 144, 102810."},{"cite":"Oktaviana, A. A. et al. (2024). Narrative cave art in Indonesia by 51,200 years ago. Nature 631, 814–818.","doi":"10.1038/s41586-024-07541-7"}]},{"id":"molecules-that-remember","title":"Molecules that remember","start":50000,"end":50000,"camera":{"sites":["denisova-cave"],"zoom":3.5},"clock":true,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Bones and stones are not the only archive. Since the 1990s, molecules have become a second one. In 1997 Svante Pääbo’s laboratory in Munich recovered mitochondrial DNA from the Neanderthal fossil found in 1856 in the Neander valley. In 2010 his team in Leipzig published a draft Neanderthal genome, and the same year identified a wholly new human group, the Denisovans, from the DNA of a finger bone found in a Siberian cave. Pääbo received the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 2022.","sites":["denisova-cave"],"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"feldhofer-skull","move":"push","caption":"Neanderthal 1, found in 1856 in the Neander valley"},{"at":2,"type":"photo","media":"paabo","move":"still","lower":{"name":"Svante Pääbo","sub":"Max Planck Institute for Evolutionary Anthropology, Leipzig"}},{"at":3,"type":"photo","media":"denisova-cave-photo","move":"pan-left","caption":"Denisova Cave in the Altai, where a finger bone revealed a new human group"},{"at":4,"type":"photo","media":"mpi-leipzig","move":"push","caption":"The Max Planck Institute for Evolutionary Anthropology, Leipzig"}],"sentences":["Bones and stones are not the only archive.","Since the 1990s, molecules have become a second one.","In 1997 Svante Pääbo’s laboratory in Munich recovered mitochondrial DNA from the Neanderthal fossil found in 1856 in the Neander valley.","In 2010 his team in Leipzig published a draft Neanderthal genome, and the same year identified a wholly new human group, the Denisovans, from the DNA of a finger bone found in a Siberian cave.","Pääbo received the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 2022."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"DNA breaks into ever shorter fragments and survives best in cold, dry places and in the dense petrous bone of the inner ear. The oldest human DNA sequenced so far comes from the Sima de los Huesos in Spain, about 430,000 years old. Beyond people, a mammoth tooth from Siberian permafrost yielded DNA more than a million years old in 2021, and in 2022 Eske Willerslev’s team read the DNA of an entire ecosystem, mastodons, reindeer and birch, from two-million-year-old sediment at Kap København in northern Greenland.","sites":["sima-de-los-huesos","kap-kobenhavn"],"camera":{"sites":["sima-de-los-huesos","kap-kobenhavn"]},"time":430000,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"clean-lab","move":"push","caption":"A clean laboratory for ancient DNA"},{"at":1,"type":"photo","media":"sima-skull-5","move":"pan-right","caption":"Skull 5 from the Sima de los Huesos, Atapuerca, about 430,000 years old"},{"at":2,"type":"montage","media":["mammoth-tooth","kap-kobenhavn"],"captions":["A mammoth tooth from Siberian permafrost: DNA more than a million years old","Kap København, northern Greenland: DNA from two-million-year-old sediment"]}],"sentences":["DNA breaks into ever shorter fragments and survives best in cold, dry places and in the dense petrous bone of the inner ear.","The oldest human DNA sequenced so far comes from the Sima de los Huesos in Spain, about 430,000 years old.","Beyond people, a mammoth tooth from Siberian permafrost yielded DNA more than a million years old in 2021, and in 2022 Eske Willerslev’s team read the DNA of an entire ecosystem, mastodons, reindeer and birch, from two-million-year-old sediment at Kap København in northern Greenland."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"DNA no longer needs a bone. In 2017 Viviane Slon and colleagues extracted Neanderthal and Denisovan DNA from the dirt of cave floors, and sediment DNA has since revealed who lived in caves that never yielded a fossil.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"denisova-cave-photo","move":"tilt-up","caption":"Cave floors hold DNA without bones"}],"sentences":["DNA no longer needs a bone.","In 2017 Viviane Slon and colleagues extracted Neanderthal and Denisovan DNA from the dirt of cave floors, and sediment DNA has since revealed who lived in caves that never yielded a fossil."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Proteins last longer than DNA. In 2020 enamel proteins placed Homo antecessor, about 800,000 years old, on the family tree. In 2025 Palesa Madupe and colleagues read enamel proteins from four Paranthropus teeth about two million years old at Swartkrans in South Africa and told which were male and which female, and Daniel Green’s team recovered protein fragments up to 18 million years old from fossil mammals of the Turkana Basin.","sites":["swartkrans"],"camera":{"sites":["swartkrans"],"zoom":4},"time":2000000,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"antecessor","move":"push","caption":"Homo antecessor, Gran Dolina, about 800,000 years old"},{"at":2,"type":"photo","media":"sk48","move":"pan-right","caption":"SK 48, Paranthropus robustus from Swartkrans, South Africa"}],"sentences":["Proteins last longer than DNA.","In 2020 enamel proteins placed Homo antecessor, about 800,000 years old, on the family tree.","In 2025 Palesa Madupe and colleagues read enamel proteins from four Paranthropus teeth about two million years old at Swartkrans in South Africa and told which were male and which female, and Daniel Green’s team recovered protein fragments up to 18 million years old from fossil mammals of the Turkana Basin."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"In June 2025 proteins and a scrap of mitochondrial DNA from dental plaque tied the almost complete Harbin skull from northeast China, at least 146,000 years old, to the Denisovans, and proteins identified a jaw dredged from the seabed off Taiwan as a Denisovan man. A group known for fifteen years almost only from its genes had been given a face.","sites":["harbin","penghu-channel"],"camera":{"sites":["harbin","penghu-channel","denisova-cave"]},"time":146000,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"harbin-skull","move":"push","caption":"The Harbin cranium, at least 146,000 years old, now a Denisovan"},{"at":1,"type":"photo","media":"penghu","move":"pan-left","caption":"The Penghu jaw, dredged from the Taiwan Strait: a Denisovan man"}],"sentences":["In June 2025 proteins and a scrap of mitochondrial DNA from dental plaque tied the almost complete Harbin skull from northeast China, at least 146,000 years old, to the Denisovans, and proteins identified a jaw dredged from the seabed off Taiwan as a Denisovan man.","A group known for fifteen years almost only from its genes had been given a face."]},{"speaker":"research","text":"How far can molecules reach? Researchers in palaeoproteomics expect enamel proteins to help sort out relationships among hominins that will never yield DNA. Others caution that enamel preserves only a handful of proteins, so each fragment carries few informative differences; that contamination and chemical damage are hard to rule out; and that hominin DNA has not been recovered from anything older than about 430,000 years, with warm climates destroying it far faster.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"split","question":"How far back can molecules take us?","views":[{"label":"A deep molecular record","text":"Enamel proteins now reach 2 million years for hominins and 18 million for other mammals; proteomics may sort out early hominin relationships that anatomy cannot.","media":"sk48"},{"label":"A thin signal","text":"Few proteins survive in enamel, each with few informative differences; contamination and damage are hard to exclude; DNA stops at about 430,000 years for humans.","media":"harbin-skull"}],"status":"Proteins reliably give sex and broad relationships for some species older than 2 million years; detailed family trees still rest mainly on anatomy.","stagger":8}],"sentences":["How far can molecules reach?","Researchers in palaeoproteomics expect enamel proteins to help sort out relationships among hominins that will never yield DNA.","Others caution that enamel preserves only a handful of proteins, so each fragment carries few informative differences; that contamination and chemical damage are hard to rule out; and that hominin DNA has not been recovered from anything older than about 430,000 years, with warm climates destroying it far faster."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Chemistry also tells us what people ate and where they grew up. Carbon isotopes in tooth enamel separate diets of leaves and fruit from diets of grasses and sedges; nitrogen tracks meat; strontium matches a tooth to the geology of the place where its owner spent childhood, and so reveals who moved.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"mass-spectrometer","move":"push","caption":"Isotopes in tooth enamel record diet and childhood home"}],"sentences":["Chemistry also tells us what people ate and where they grew up.","Carbon isotopes in tooth enamel separate diets of leaves and fruit from diets of grasses and sedges; nitrogen tracks meat; strontium matches a tooth to the geology of the place where its owner spent childhood, and so reveals who moved."]}],"sources":[{"cite":"Krings, M. et al. (1997). Neandertal DNA sequences and the origin of modern humans. Cell 90, 19–30."},{"cite":"Green, R. E. et al. (2010). A draft sequence of the Neandertal genome. Science 328, 710–722.","doi":"10.1126/science.1188021"},{"cite":"Meyer, M. et al. (2016). Nuclear DNA sequences from the Middle Pleistocene Sima de los Huesos hominins. Nature 531, 504–507."},{"cite":"van der Valk, T. et al. (2021). Million-year-old DNA sheds light on the genomic history of mammoths. Nature 591, 265–269."},{"cite":"Kjær, K. H. et al. (2022). A 2-million-year-old ecosystem in Greenland uncovered by environmental DNA. Nature 612, 283–291.","doi":"10.1038/s41586-022-05453-y"},{"cite":"Slon, V. et al. (2017). Neandertal and Denisovan DNA from Pleistocene sediments. Science 356, 605–608."},{"cite":"Welker, F. et al. (2020). The dental proteome of Homo antecessor. Nature 580, 235–238."},{"cite":"Madupe, P. P. et al. (2025). Enamel proteins reveal biological sex and genetic variability in southern African Paranthropus. Science 388, 969–973.","doi":"10.1126/science.adt9539"},{"cite":"Green, D. R. et al. (2025). Eighteen million years of diverse enamel proteomes from the East African Rift. Nature.","doi":"10.1038/s41586-025-09040-9"},{"cite":"Fu, Q. et al. (2025). The proteome of the late Middle Pleistocene Harbin individual. Science 388.","doi":"10.1126/science.adu9677"},{"cite":"Fu, Q. et al. (2025). Denisovan mitochondrial DNA from dental calculus of the more than 146,000-year-old Harbin cranium. Cell 188."},{"cite":"Tsutaya, T. et al. (2025). A male Denisovan mandible from Pleistocene Taiwan. Science 388, 176–180.","doi":"10.1126/science.ads3888"}]},{"id":"archives-of-climate","title":"Archives of climate","start":1250000,"end":700000,"camera":{"sites":["little-dome-c"],"zoom":2.2},"clock":true,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","text":"The people in this story lived through climates that swung wildly. We know because the ocean floor and the ice remember. Tiny shelled creatures called foraminifera build their shells from seawater, and the ratio of oxygen isotopes in those shells tracks how much water was locked up in ice. In 2005 Lorraine Lisiecki and Maureen Raymo stacked 57 deep-sea records into a single curve covering 5.3 million years. Its numbered warm and cold stages, odd for warm, even for cold, are the calendar of the ice ages, and the sea-level curve along the bottom of this screen is built from records like it.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"joides-resolution","move":"push","caption":"The drilling ship JOIDES Resolution, which recovered deep-sea sediment cores for decades"},{"at":2,"type":"photo","media":"foraminifera","move":"pan-right","caption":"Foraminifera: shells whose chemistry records the ice"},{"at":3,"type":"photo","media":"sediment-core","move":"tilt-up","caption":"A deep-sea sediment core"},{"at":4,"type":"diagram","diagram":"sealevel","params":{"title":"Sea level through the ice ages, from the LR04 stack and coral records","from":800000,"to":2000,"secs":14,"marks":[{"year":125000,"label":"Last interglacial"},{"year":20000,"label":"Last Glacial Maximum"}]}}],"sentences":["The people in this story lived through climates that swung wildly.","We know because the ocean floor and the ice remember.","Tiny shelled creatures called foraminifera build their shells from seawater, and the ratio of oxygen isotopes in those shells tracks how much water was locked up in ice.","In 2005 Lorraine Lisiecki and Maureen Raymo stacked 57 deep-sea records into a single curve covering 5.3 million years.","Its numbered warm and cold stages, odd for warm, even for cold, are the calendar of the ice ages, and the sea-level curve along the bottom of this screen is built from records like it."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Antarctic ice holds a more direct record: bubbles of ancient air. A European project at Dome C recovered 800,000 years of carbon dioxide and temperature. In January 2025 its successor, Beyond EPICA, reached bedrock at nearby Little Dome C with a core 2,800 metres long whose ice is more than 1.2 million years old. The last samples reached European laboratories in April 2026, and the analysis is under way.","sites":["little-dome-c"],"time":1200000,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"ice-core-section","move":"push","caption":"A section of ice core: bubbles of ancient air"},{"at":1,"type":"photo","media":"concordia","move":"pan-left","caption":"Concordia Station at Dome C, Antarctica"},{"at":2,"type":"photo","media":"beyond-epica-drill","move":"push","caption":"Beyond EPICA drilling at Little Dome C"},{"at":3,"type":"number","value":"1.2 million years","label":"The oldest continuous ice core, reached at Little Dome C in January 2025","kicker":"Beyond EPICA"}],"sentences":["Antarctic ice holds a more direct record: bubbles of ancient air.","A European project at Dome C recovered 800,000 years of carbon dioxide and temperature.","In January 2025 its successor, Beyond EPICA, reached bedrock at nearby Little Dome C with a core 2,800 metres long whose ice is more than 1.2 million years old.","The last samples reached European laboratories in April 2026, and the analysis is under way."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"That core targets a puzzle. Between about 1.25 million and 700,000 years ago the rhythm of the ice ages changed: cycles of roughly 41,000 years, set by the tilt of Earth’s axis, gave way to longer and deeper cycles of about 100,000 years. The change is called the Mid-Pleistocene Transition, and nobody yet agrees why it happened. Proposals include the slow scouring of loose sediment from beneath the northern ice sheets, which let them grow thicker and more stable, and a long-term decline in carbon dioxide. Hominins had already lived in Europe and Asia for a million years when it began.","time":700000,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"type","text":"Cycles of about 41,000 years gave way to cycles of about 100,000 years.","kicker":"The Mid-Pleistocene Transition, about 1.25 million to 700,000 years ago"},{"at":3,"type":"photo","media":"greenland-ice-aerial","move":"pan-right","caption":"Ice sheets grew thicker and more stable"},{"at":4,"type":"globe","camera":{"box":[-10,30,120,60]}}],"sentences":["That core targets a puzzle.","Between about 1.25 million and 700,000 years ago the rhythm of the ice ages changed: cycles of roughly 41,000 years, set by the tilt of Earth’s axis, gave way to longer and deeper cycles of about 100,000 years.","The change is called the Mid-Pleistocene Transition, and nobody yet agrees why it happened.","Proposals include the slow scouring of loose sediment from beneath the northern ice sheets, which let them grow thicker and more stable, and a long-term decline in carbon dioxide.","Hominins had already lived in Europe and Asia for a million years when it began."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"In Africa the swings showed less as ice than as rain. The strength of the monsoon follows the wobble of Earth’s axis, about every 21,000 years, turning the Sahara green and then to desert again and again. Lake cores, stalagmites, pollen and fossil soils fill in the local detail. The climate archives give the Stone Age its weather; the sites give it its people.","camera":{"box":[-20,0,50,35]},"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"sahara-satellite","move":"pull","caption":"The Sahara from space"},{"at":1,"type":"photo","media":"tassili","move":"push","caption":"Rock art at Tassili n’Ajjer, Algeria, from a greener Sahara"},{"at":2,"type":"photo","media":"lake-yoa","move":"pan-left","caption":"Lake Yoa, Chad, whose sediments record the drying of the Sahara"},{"at":3,"type":"globe","camera":{"lon":20,"lat":15,"zoom":1}}],"sentences":["In Africa the swings showed less as ice than as rain.","The strength of the monsoon follows the wobble of Earth’s axis, about every 21,000 years, turning the Sahara green and then to desert again and again.","Lake cores, stalagmites, pollen and fossil soils fill in the local detail.","The climate archives give the Stone Age its weather; the sites give it its people."]}],"sources":[{"cite":"Lisiecki, L. E. & Raymo, M. E. (2005). A Pliocene–Pleistocene stack of 57 globally distributed benthic δ18O records. Paleoceanography 20, PA1003.","doi":"10.1029/2004PA001071"},{"cite":"EPICA community members (2004). Eight glacial cycles from an Antarctic ice core. Nature 429, 623–628."},{"cite":"Beyond EPICA – Oldest Ice (2025). Press release, 9 January 2025: Ice core more than 1.2 million years old drilled at Little Dome C. beyondepica.eu."},{"cite":"Clark, P. U. et al. (2006). The middle Pleistocene transition: characteristics, mechanisms, and implications for long-term changes in atmospheric pCO2. Quaternary Science Reviews 25, 3150–3184."}]},{"id":"the-world-at-the-ice-maximum","title":"The world at the ice maximum","start":26000,"end":19000,"camera":{"lon":0,"lat":45,"zoom":1},"clock":true,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Twenty thousand years ago, at the Last Glacial Maximum, you could have walked from England to Denmark, from Siberia to Alaska, and from New Guinea to Tasmania. Ice sheets up to three kilometres thick covered most of Canada and Scandinavia. So much water was locked up in them that the sea stood about 120 to 130 metres lower than today. Watch the coastlines on this globe as the timeline moves: the shelves that appear were dry land, and people lived on them.","time":21000,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"lgm-map","move":"pull","caption":"The world at the Last Glacial Maximum: ice sheets and exposed shelves","fit":"contain"},{"at":1,"type":"photo","media":"greenland-ice-aerial","move":"pan-right","caption":"Greenland’s ice sheet today; Canada and Scandinavia looked like this"},{"at":2,"type":"photo","media":"vatnajokull","move":"push","caption":"Vatnajökull, Iceland"},{"at":3,"type":"globe","camera":{"lon":0,"lat":45,"zoom":1}}],"sentences":["Twenty thousand years ago, at the Last Glacial Maximum, you could have walked from England to Denmark, from Siberia to Alaska, and from New Guinea to Tasmania.","Ice sheets up to three kilometres thick covered most of Canada and Scandinavia.","So much water was locked up in them that the sea stood about 120 to 130 metres lower than today.","Watch the coastlines on this globe as the timeline moves: the shelves that appear were dry land, and people lived on them."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"A 2020 study led by Jessica Tierney estimated that the world was about six degrees colder than before industrial times, with carbon dioxide near 190 parts per million, against more than 420 today. It was drier and dustier; tropical forests shrank and deserts grew.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"number","value":"6 degrees colder","label":"Global mean temperature at the Last Glacial Maximum, compared with pre-industrial times","kicker":"Tierney and colleagues, Nature, 2020"},{"at":1,"type":"photo","media":"mammoth-steppe","move":"pan-left","caption":"The mammoth steppe: dry, dusty and grassy (reconstruction)"}],"sentences":["A 2020 study led by Jessica Tierney estimated that the world was about six degrees colder than before industrial times, with carbon dioxide near 190 parts per million, against more than 420 today.","It was drier and dustier; tropical forests shrank and deserts grew."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"The drowned lowlands have names we gave them afterwards. Beringia joined Siberia to Alaska. Sundaland joined Sumatra, Java and Borneo to the Asian mainland, a lowland of more than a million square kilometres. Sahul united Australia, New Guinea and Tasmania. Doggerland joined Britain to the continent, and the floor of the Persian Gulf was a river valley.","sites":["bering-land-bridge","sunda-shelf","bass-strait","dogger-bank"],"camera":{"lon":100,"lat":20,"zoom":1},"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"globe","camera":{"lon":100,"lat":20,"zoom":1}},{"at":1,"type":"photo","media":"beringia-map","move":"pull","caption":"Beringia","fit":"contain"},{"at":2,"type":"photo","media":"sundaland-map","move":"pull","caption":"Sundaland","fit":"contain"},{"at":3,"type":"photo","media":"sahul-map","move":"pull","caption":"Sahul","fit":"contain"},{"at":4,"type":"photo","media":"doggerland-map","move":"pull","caption":"Doggerland","fit":"contain"}],"sentences":["The drowned lowlands have names we gave them afterwards.","Beringia joined Siberia to Alaska.","Sundaland joined Sumatra, Java and Borneo to the Asian mainland, a lowland of more than a million square kilometres.","Sahul united Australia, New Guinea and Tasmania.","Doggerland joined Britain to the continent, and the floor of the Persian Gulf was a river valley."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Every one of those lands is now sea floor, and the archaeology of the people who lived on them is mostly lost, or reachable only by dredging and seismic survey. That loss shapes everything we think we know about coastlines and boats, and it will come up on every continent in this series.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"dogger-bank","move":"push","caption":"The North Sea over the Dogger Bank, once dry land"},{"at":1,"type":"globe","camera":{"lon":0,"lat":45,"zoom":1}}],"sentences":["Every one of those lands is now sea floor, and the archaeology of the people who lived on them is mostly lost, or reachable only by dredging and seismic survey.","That loss shapes everything we think we know about coastlines and boats, and it will come up on every continent in this series."]}],"sources":[{"cite":"Lambeck, K. et al. (2014). Sea level and global ice volumes from the Last Glacial Maximum to the Holocene. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 111, 15296–15303.","doi":"10.1073/pnas.1411762111"},{"cite":"Tierney, J. E. et al. (2020). Glacial cooling and climate sensitivity revisited. Nature 584, 569–573.","doi":"10.1038/s41586-020-2617-x"},{"cite":"Clark, P. U. et al. (2009). The Last Glacial Maximum. Science 325, 710–714."}]},{"id":"the-great-thaw","title":"The great thaw","start":19000,"end":8000,"camera":{"sites":["northgrip"],"zoom":2},"clock":true,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Then the ice began to melt, but not smoothly. Deglaciation was paced by changes in Earth’s orbit that increased northern summer sunshine from about 20,000 years ago, amplified by rising carbon dioxide. The path was jagged because meltwater disturbed the Atlantic circulation that carries heat north.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"vatnajokull","move":"push","caption":"Meltwater from a retreating glacier, Iceland"},{"at":2,"type":"globe","camera":{"sites":["northgrip"],"zoom":2}}],"sentences":["Then the ice began to melt, but not smoothly.","Deglaciation was paced by changes in Earth’s orbit that increased northern summer sunshine from about 20,000 years ago, amplified by rising carbon dioxide.","The path was jagged because meltwater disturbed the Atlantic circulation that carries heat north."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"About 14,700 years ago the circulation strengthened and Greenland warmed by around ten degrees within decades. The sea surged: corals drilled off Tahiti record a rise of some 14 to 18 metres in about three and a half centuries, four centimetres a year.","time":14700,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"number","value":"10 degrees in decades","label":"The warming over Greenland at the start of the Bølling interstadial, about 14,700 years ago","kicker":"Greenland ice cores"},{"at":1,"type":"photo","media":"tahiti-reef","move":"pan-right","caption":"Corals off Tahiti record the sea rising four centimetres a year"}],"sentences":["About 14,700 years ago the circulation strengthened and Greenland warmed by around ten degrees within decades.","The sea surged: corals drilled off Tahiti record a rise of some 14 to 18 metres in about three and a half centuries, four centimetres a year."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Around 12,900 years ago the North Atlantic world plunged back into near-glacial cold for twelve centuries, a relapse called the Younger Dryas after an Arctic flower whose remains reappear in Scandinavian lake mud. When it ended, 11,700 years ago, the Holocene began, the epoch in which every farming society has lived. Its start is formally defined in a Greenland ice core, at a depth of 1,492 metres, where a team led by Jørgen Peder Steffensen showed that the change took only a few years.","sites":["northgrip"],"time":11700,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"dryas-flower","move":"push","caption":"Dryas octopetala, the mountain avens that named the cold relapse"},{"at":1,"type":"photo","media":"ngrip-drilling","move":"pan-left","caption":"Drilling the NorthGRIP core in Greenland"},{"at":2,"type":"photo","media":"ice-core-section","move":"tilt-up","caption":"The base of the Holocene is defined at 1,492 metres depth in this core"}],"sentences":["Around 12,900 years ago the North Atlantic world plunged back into near-glacial cold for twelve centuries, a relapse called the Younger Dryas after an Arctic flower whose remains reappear in Scandinavian lake mud.","When it ended, 11,700 years ago, the Holocene began, the epoch in which every farming society has lived.","Its start is formally defined in a Greenland ice core, at a depth of 1,492 metres, where a team led by Jørgen Peder Steffensen showed that the change took only a few years."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"The rising water drowned whole landscapes. Around 8,150 years ago a vast undersea landslide off Norway, the Storegga slide, sent a tsunami across the North Sea that left sand several metres above the shorelines of the time; Vince Gaffney’s team has argued that it struck a Doggerland already breaking into islands. Sundaland went under; Tasmania and then New Guinea became islands. Patrick Nunn and Nicholas Reid have argued that some Aboriginal Australian stories preserve memories of coastlines lost more than 7,000 years ago; sceptics reply that people can infer past landscapes from what they see today, and that dating a story is hard.","sites":["shetland-storegga","dogger-bank"],"camera":{"sites":["shetland-storegga","dogger-bank"]},"time":8150,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"globe","camera":{"sites":["shetland-storegga","dogger-bank"]}},{"at":1,"type":"photo","media":"storegga-map","move":"pull","caption":"The Storegga slide and the reach of its tsunami","fit":"contain"},{"at":2,"type":"globe","camera":{"box":[110,-45,155,-5]}},{"at":3,"type":"photo","media":"spencer-gulf","move":"push","caption":"Spencer Gulf, South Australia, one of the drowned coasts in Aboriginal stories"}],"sentences":["The rising water drowned whole landscapes.","Around 8,150 years ago a vast undersea landslide off Norway, the Storegga slide, sent a tsunami across the North Sea that left sand several metres above the shorelines of the time; Vince Gaffney’s team has argued that it struck a Doggerland already breaking into islands.","Sundaland went under; Tasmania and then New Guinea became islands.","Patrick Nunn and Nicholas Reid have argued that some Aboriginal Australian stories preserve memories of coastlines lost more than 7,000 years ago; sceptics reply that people can infer past landscapes from what they see today, and that dating a story is hard."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Meanwhile the Sahara turned green, with lakes, hippos and grasslands. People everywhere adapted: fishing, gathering seeds and nuts, and staying longer in one place. The great thaw is the stage on which farming, villages and metal will appear in later episodes.","camera":{"box":[-20,0,50,35]},"time":8000,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"tassili","move":"pan-right","caption":"A greener Sahara: cattle and herders at Tassili n’Ajjer"},{"at":1,"type":"photo","media":"lake-chad","move":"pull","caption":"Lake Chad, remnant of a lake once the size of the Caspian Sea"},{"at":2,"type":"globe","camera":{"lon":20,"lat":15,"zoom":1}}],"sentences":["Meanwhile the Sahara turned green, with lakes, hippos and grasslands.","People everywhere adapted: fishing, gathering seeds and nuts, and staying longer in one place.","The great thaw is the stage on which farming, villages and metal will appear in later episodes."]}],"sources":[{"cite":"Deschamps, P. et al. (2012). Ice-sheet collapse and sea-level rise at the Bølling warming 14,600 years ago. Nature 483, 559–564."},{"cite":"Steffensen, J. P. et al. (2008). High-resolution Greenland ice core data show abrupt climate change happens in few years. Science 321, 680–684."},{"cite":"Walker, M. et al. (2009). Formal definition and dating of the GSSP for the base of the Holocene using the Greenland NorthGRIP ice core. Journal of Quaternary Science 24, 3–17."},{"cite":"Gaffney, V. et al. (2020). Multi-proxy characterisation of the Storegga tsunami and its impact on the early Holocene landscapes of the southern North Sea. Geosciences 10, 270."},{"cite":"Nunn, P. D. & Reid, N. J. (2016). Aboriginal memories of inundation of the Australian coast dating from more than 7000 years ago. Australian Geographer 47, 11–47."}]},{"id":"what-ended-the-warmth","title":"What ended the warmth?","start":12900,"end":11700,"camera":{"sites":["lake-agassiz"],"zoom":2.4},"clock":true,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","text":"What caused the Younger Dryas? The question has produced one of the longest arguments in Quaternary science, and one that has crossed into popular culture. Here are the positions.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"lake-agassiz-map","move":"pull","caption":"Glacial Lake Agassiz at its greatest extent","fit":"contain"},{"at":2,"type":"type","text":"Here are the positions.","kicker":"Two explanations"}],"sentences":["What caused the Younger Dryas?","The question has produced one of the longest arguments in Quaternary science, and one that has crossed into popular culture.","Here are the positions."]},{"speaker":"research","text":"The most widely held explanation goes back to Wallace Broecker in 1989. As the Laurentide ice sheet retreated, meltwater pooled in glacial Lake Agassiz, larger than any lake today, and a sudden outflow to the ocean freshened the North Atlantic and weakened the circulation that warms Europe. For decades the flood path was elusive; Broecker himself wrote in 2006 that the failure to find it was disconcerting. In 2010 Julian Murton and colleagues identified gravels and an eroded surface along the Mackenzie River, pointing to a drainage north into the Arctic Ocean, and in 2018 Lloyd Keigwin’s team found the freshening recorded in sediment cores off the Mackenzie delta. Whether that water alone could cool the region for twelve centuries is still debated; a weakened circulation sustained by further freshwater is a common answer.","sites":["lake-agassiz"],"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"broecker","move":"still","lower":{"name":"Wallace Broecker","sub":"Lamont-Doherty Earth Observatory"}},{"at":1,"type":"photo","media":"lake-agassiz-map","move":"push","caption":"Lake Agassiz, larger than any lake today","fit":"contain"},{"at":3,"type":"photo","media":"mackenzie-delta","move":"pan-right","caption":"The Mackenzie delta, where the flood reached the Arctic Ocean"},{"at":4,"type":"globe","camera":{"sites":["lake-agassiz"],"zoom":2.4}}],"sentences":["The most widely held explanation goes back to Wallace Broecker in 1989.","As the Laurentide ice sheet retreated, meltwater pooled in glacial Lake Agassiz, larger than any lake today, and a sudden outflow to the ocean freshened the North Atlantic and weakened the circulation that warms Europe.","For decades the flood path was elusive; Broecker himself wrote in 2006 that the failure to find it was disconcerting.","In 2010 Julian Murton and colleagues identified gravels and an eroded surface along the Mackenzie River, pointing to a drainage north into the Arctic Ocean, and in 2018 Lloyd Keigwin’s team found the freshening recorded in sediment cores off the Mackenzie delta.","Whether that water alone could cool the region for twelve centuries is still debated; a weakened circulation sustained by further freshwater is a common answer."]},{"speaker":"research","text":"A rival explanation was proposed in 2007 by Richard Firestone, James Kennett and colleagues: that fragments of a comet exploded over or struck North America about 12,900 years ago, igniting wildfires, destabilising the ice sheet, and contributing to the extinction of the mammoths and the end of the Clovis culture. Its proponents, organised as the Comet Research Group, point to a layer they call the Younger Dryas boundary: magnetic microspherules, nanodiamonds, melted glass and a platinum spike found at many sites, including a platinum peak in Greenland ice reported by Michail Petaev in 2013, and meltglass at the village of Abu Hureyra in Syria described by Andrew Moore in 2020. Martin Sweatman’s 2021 review concluded that the evidence had been replicated widely enough to be taken seriously.","sites":["murray-springs","abu-hureyra"],"camera":{"sites":["murray-springs","lake-agassiz"],"zoom":2.2},"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"kennett","move":"still","lower":{"name":"James Kennett","sub":"University of California, Santa Barbara"}},{"at":1,"type":"montage","media":["black-mat","clovis-point"],"captions":["The black mat at Murray Springs, Arizona, above the last mammoth bones","A Clovis point"]},{"at":2,"type":"photo","media":"abu-hureyra","move":"push","caption":"Abu Hureyra, Syria, excavated before the Tabqa reservoir covered it"}],"sentences":["A rival explanation was proposed in 2007 by Richard Firestone, James Kennett and colleagues: that fragments of a comet exploded over or struck North America about 12,900 years ago, igniting wildfires, destabilising the ice sheet, and contributing to the extinction of the mammoths and the end of the Clovis culture.","Its proponents, organised as the Comet Research Group, point to a layer they call the Younger Dryas boundary: magnetic microspherules, nanodiamonds, melted glass and a platinum spike found at many sites, including a platinum peak in Greenland ice reported by Michail Petaev in 2013, and meltglass at the village of Abu Hureyra in Syria described by Andrew Moore in 2020.","Martin Sweatman’s 2021 review concluded that the evidence had been replicated widely enough to be taken seriously."]},{"speaker":"research","text":"Critics answer on several fronts. Independent teams failed to find nanodiamonds or spherules at the same levels, or found them at many other levels too. A 2014 study led by David Meltzer argued that most of the claimed boundary layers were not securely dated to 12,900 years. No crater of the right age is known: the Hiawatha crater under the Greenland ice, once a candidate, was dated in 2022 to 58 million years. And the mammoths and the Clovis culture show no sudden break. In 2023 Vance Holliday and twelve co-authors published a long review in Earth-Science Reviews under the title of a comprehensive refutation. Proponents replied in 2024 that the review misrepresented their studies, and Holliday’s group rebutted in turn.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"split","question":"What caused the Younger Dryas?","views":[{"label":"Meltwater","text":"Broecker, 1989; Murton and colleagues, 2010; Keigwin and colleagues, 2018: Lake Agassiz drained north through the Mackenzie valley, freshened the Arctic and North Atlantic and weakened the ocean circulation. The majority view."},{"label":"A cosmic impact","text":"Firestone, Kennett and colleagues, 2007; Petaev, 2013; Moore, 2020; Sweatman, 2021: microspherules, nanodiamonds, meltglass and platinum at a boundary layer on several continents mark a comet airburst."},{"label":"The critics","text":"Meltzer and colleagues, 2014: the layers are not securely dated. Holliday and 12 colleagues, 2023: the markers are not unique to impacts, no crater exists, and the extinctions and Clovis show no sudden break."}],"status":"Most Quaternary scientists favour meltwater and regard the impact as unproven; its proponents regard it as prematurely dismissed.","stagger":10}],"sentences":["Critics answer on several fronts.","Independent teams failed to find nanodiamonds or spherules at the same levels, or found them at many other levels too.","A 2014 study led by David Meltzer argued that most of the claimed boundary layers were not securely dated to 12,900 years.","No crater of the right age is known: the Hiawatha crater under the Greenland ice, once a candidate, was dated in 2022 to 58 million years.","And the mammoths and the Clovis culture show no sudden break.","In 2023 Vance Holliday and twelve co-authors published a long review in Earth-Science Reviews under the title of a comprehensive refutation.","Proponents replied in 2024 that the review misrepresented their studies, and Holliday’s group rebutted in turn."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"The argument has since moved into the record of retractions. In 2025 Scientific Reports retracted a Comet Research Group paper claiming that an airburst destroyed the Bronze Age city of Tall el-Hammam in Jordan, citing problems with its data. In February 2026 the journal PLOS One retracted two 2025 papers by group members, one on shocked quartz in North America and one on sediment cores from Baffin Bay, citing incomplete sampling, an age model that mixed dates from centuries on either side of the boundary, and, in the Baffin Bay case, particles presented as cosmic spherules that were the shells of single-celled marine organisms. The group disputes the retractions and continues to publish, partly in a journal of its own.","camera":{"sites":["abu-hureyra"],"zoom":3.5},"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"tall-el-hammam","move":"push","caption":"Tall el-Hammam, Jordan: the 2021 airburst paper was retracted in 2025"},{"at":2,"type":"photo","media":"baffin-bay","move":"pan-left","caption":"Baffin Bay: the 2025 sediment core paper was retracted in 2026"},{"at":3,"type":"type","text":"The group disputes the retractions and continues to publish.","kicker":"2025 to 2026"}],"sentences":["The argument has since moved into the record of retractions.","In 2025 Scientific Reports retracted a Comet Research Group paper claiming that an airburst destroyed the Bronze Age city of Tall el-Hammam in Jordan, citing problems with its data.","In February 2026 the journal PLOS One retracted two 2025 papers by group members, one on shocked quartz in North America and one on sediment cores from Baffin Bay, citing incomplete sampling, an age model that mixed dates from centuries on either side of the boundary, and, in the Baffin Bay case, particles presented as cosmic spherules that were the shells of single-celled marine organisms.","The group disputes the retractions and continues to publish, partly in a journal of its own."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Where does the field stand? Most Quaternary scientists regard the meltwater explanation as the best supported, and the impact hypothesis as unproven. Its proponents regard it as prematurely dismissed. The lost-civilisation reading of the same event, popularised by Graham Hancock, is a further step, which we will examine alongside Göbekli Tepe in episode 5. In this series each claim is weighed by its evidence, and the evidence is what you have just heard.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"type","text":"Most Quaternary scientists favour meltwater and regard the impact as unproven. Its proponents regard it as prematurely dismissed.","kicker":"Where it stands"},{"at":3,"type":"photo","media":"gobekli-pillars","move":"push","caption":"Göbekli Tepe, where the lost-civilisation reading is examined in episode 5"}],"sentences":["Where does the field stand?","Most Quaternary scientists regard the meltwater explanation as the best supported, and the impact hypothesis as unproven.","Its proponents regard it as prematurely dismissed.","The lost-civilisation reading of the same event, popularised by Graham Hancock, is a further step, which we will examine alongside Göbekli Tepe in episode 5.","In this series each claim is weighed by its evidence, and the evidence is what you have just heard."]}],"sources":[{"cite":"Broecker, W. S. et al. (1989). Routing of meltwater from the Laurentide Ice Sheet during the Younger Dryas cold episode. Nature 341, 318–321."},{"cite":"Murton, J. B., Bateman, M. D., Dallimore, S. R., Teller, J. T. & Yang, Z. (2010). Identification of Younger Dryas outburst flood path from Lake Agassiz to the Arctic Ocean. Nature 464, 740–743.","doi":"10.1038/nature08954"},{"cite":"Keigwin, L. D. et al. (2018). Deglacial floods in the Beaufort Sea preceded Younger Dryas cooling. Nature Geoscience 11, 599–604."},{"cite":"Firestone, R. B. et al. (2007). Evidence for an extraterrestrial impact 12,900 years ago that contributed to the megafaunal extinctions and the Younger Dryas cooling. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 104, 16016–16021."},{"cite":"Petaev, M. I., Huang, S., Jacobsen, S. B. & Zindler, A. (2013). Large Pt anomaly in the Greenland ice core points to a cataclysm at the onset of Younger Dryas. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 110, 12917–12920."},{"cite":"Moore, A. M. T. et al. (2020). Evidence of cosmic impact at Abu Hureyra, Syria at the Younger Dryas onset: high-temperature melting at more than 2200 °C. Scientific Reports 10, 4185."},{"cite":"Sweatman, M. B. (2021). The Younger Dryas impact hypothesis: review of the impact evidence. Earth-Science Reviews 218, 103677."},{"cite":"Meltzer, D. J., Holliday, V. T., Cannon, M. D. & Miller, D. S. (2014). Chronological evidence fails to support claim of an isochronous widespread layer of cosmic impact indicators dated to 12,800 years ago. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 111, E2162–E2171."},{"cite":"Kenny, G. G. et al. (2022). A Late Paleocene age for Greenland’s Hiawatha impact structure. Science Advances 8, eabm2434."},{"cite":"Holliday, V. T. et al. (2023). Comprehensive refutation of the Younger Dryas Impact Hypothesis (YDIH). Earth-Science Reviews 247, 104502.","doi":"10.1016/j.earscirev.2023.104502"},{"cite":"Powell, J. L., Sweatman, M. B. & West, A. (2024). Rejection of Holliday et al.’s alleged refutation of the Younger Dryas impact hypothesis. Earth-Science Reviews (comment and reply)."},{"cite":"Scientific Reports (2025). Retraction note: A Tunguska sized airburst destroyed Tall el-Hammam, a Middle Bronze Age city in the Jordan Valley near the Dead Sea. Scientific Reports 15."},{"cite":"PLOS One (2026). Retraction: A 12,800-year-old layer with cometary dust, microspherules, and platinum anomaly recorded in multiple cores from Baffin Bay; and Retraction: Shocked quartz at the Younger Dryas onset. PLOS One, 11 February 2026."}]},{"id":"catastrophes-on-trial","title":"Catastrophes on trial","start":930000,"end":41000,"camera":{"lon":60,"lat":15,"zoom":1},"clock":true,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Some of the most dramatic stories about our past come from single studies. Three examples show how the field tests them.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"lake-toba","move":"push","caption":"Lake Toba, Sumatra: the crater of the eruption of 74,000 years ago"}],"sentences":["Some of the most dramatic stories about our past come from single studies.","Three examples show how the field tests them."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"In 2023 Wangjie Hu, Haipeng Li and colleagues published a method called FitCoal, which reads past population size from the pattern of genetic variants in living people. Applied to more than 3,000 modern genomes, it indicated that between about 930,000 and 813,000 years ago the ancestors of modern humans fell to about 1,280 breeding individuals and stayed there for more than 100,000 years.","time":930000,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"diagram","diagram":"bottleneck","params":{"title":"A claimed bottleneck, 930,000 to 813,000 years ago","secs":8}}],"sentences":["In 2023 Wangjie Hu, Haipeng Li and colleagues published a method called FitCoal, which reads past population size from the pattern of genetic variants in living people.","Applied to more than 3,000 modern genomes, it indicated that between about 930,000 and 813,000 years ago the ancestors of modern humans fell to about 1,280 breeding individuals and stayed there for more than 100,000 years."]},{"speaker":"research","text":"Other population geneticists could not reproduce the result. In 2025 Yun Deng, Rasmus Nielsen and Yun Song argued that the bottleneck was likely a statistical artefact, and Trevor Cousins and Arun Durvasula concluded that the evidence was insufficient: established methods applied to the same genomes show nothing, a model without any bottleneck fits the data better, a population split into groups that exchanged migrants can mimic a crash, and the signal fails to appear in people outside Africa, who should carry it too. Hu and Li’s group replied in 2026 with simulations in which their method recovered sharp bottlenecks that the competing method smoothed away. The question is open; most specialists currently doubt the crash.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"split","question":"Did our ancestors nearly die out 900,000 years ago?","views":[{"label":"A severe bottleneck","text":"Hu, Li and colleagues, 2023 and 2026: FitCoal finds a drop to about 1,280 breeding individuals lasting more than 100,000 years, and recovers such events in simulations."},{"label":"A statistical artefact","text":"Deng, Nielsen and Song, 2025; Cousins and Durvasula, 2025: other methods see nothing, a model without a bottleneck fits better, population structure can mimic a crash, and non-Africans lack the signal."}],"status":"Unresolved; most population geneticists doubt the bottleneck. Ancient proteins from fossils of that age may eventually help.","stagger":8}],"sentences":["Other population geneticists could not reproduce the result.","In 2025 Yun Deng, Rasmus Nielsen and Yun Song argued that the bottleneck was likely a statistical artefact, and Trevor Cousins and Arun Durvasula concluded that the evidence was insufficient: established methods applied to the same genomes show nothing, a model without any bottleneck fits the data better, a population split into groups that exchanged migrants can mimic a crash, and the signal fails to appear in people outside Africa, who should carry it too.","Hu and Li’s group replied in 2026 with simulations in which their method recovered sharp bottlenecks that the competing method smoothed away.","The question is open; most specialists currently doubt the crash."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"About 74,000 years ago the Toba volcano in northern Sumatra erupted, one of the largest eruptions of the past two million years, and its ash fell across India. In 1998 Stanley Ambrose proposed that a volcanic winter reduced our ancestors to a few thousand adults. Archaeologists went looking. At Jwalapuram in India, Michael Petraglia’s team found the same kinds of tools below and above the ash. At Pinnacle Point in South Africa, almost 9,000 kilometres away, Eugene Smith and Curtis Marean identified microscopic shards of Toba glass in layers where occupation continued without a break. In Ethiopia, John Kappelman’s team found in 2024 that people at Shinfa-Metema adapted to longer dry seasons by fishing in shrinking pools. Genomes show no crash at that date. Toba is now widely seen as a regional disaster, not a near-extinction.","sites":["toba-caldera","jwalapuram","pinnacle-point","shinfa-metema-1"],"camera":{"sites":["toba-caldera","jwalapuram","pinnacle-point","shinfa-metema-1"]},"time":74000,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"toba-satellite","move":"pull","caption":"The Toba caldera from space"},{"at":1,"type":"photo","media":"lake-toba","move":"pan-right","caption":"The eruption was one of the largest of the past two million years"},{"at":3,"type":"photo","media":"jwalapuram","move":"push","caption":"Jwalapuram, India: tools below and above the Toba ash"},{"at":4,"type":"photo","media":"pinnacle-point","move":"pan-left","caption":"Pinnacle Point, South Africa, where life went on through the ash fall"},{"at":5,"type":"globe","camera":{"sites":["shinfa-metema-1","pinnacle-point","jwalapuram","toba-caldera"]}},{"at":7,"type":"number","value":"74,000 years ago","label":"Toba: a regional disaster, not a near-extinction","kicker":"Petraglia 2007, Smith 2018, Clarkson 2020, Kappelman 2024"}],"sentences":["About 74,000 years ago the Toba volcano in northern Sumatra erupted, one of the largest eruptions of the past two million years, and its ash fell across India.","In 1998 Stanley Ambrose proposed that a volcanic winter reduced our ancestors to a few thousand adults.","Archaeologists went looking.","At Jwalapuram in India, Michael Petraglia’s team found the same kinds of tools below and above the ash.","At Pinnacle Point in South Africa, almost 9,000 kilometres away, Eugene Smith and Curtis Marean identified microscopic shards of Toba glass in layers where occupation continued without a break.","In Ethiopia, John Kappelman’s team found in 2024 that people at Shinfa-Metema adapted to longer dry seasons by fishing in shrinking pools.","Genomes show no crash at that date.","Toba is now widely seen as a regional disaster, not a near-extinction."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"About 41,000 years ago Earth’s magnetic field weakened to a fraction of its strength and its poles wandered, an event named after lava flows at Laschamps in France. In 2021 Alan Cooper, Chris Turney and colleagues dated it precisely with radiocarbon from a kauri tree preserved in a New Zealand swamp, called the crisis the Adams Event, and linked it to megafaunal extinctions in Australia, the end of the Neanderthals and a rise in cave art. Andrea Picin, John Hawks and others replied that most of those events were already under way thousands of years earlier, and that ice cores show no marked climate change at the time. In 2025 Agnit Mukhopadhyay and colleagues modelled how auroras and radiation would have reached lower latitudes, and speculated that clothing, ochre and caves gave Homo sapiens an edge over Neanderthals. The idea is untested.","sites":["laschamps","ngawha"],"camera":{"sites":["laschamps","ngawha"]},"time":41000,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"chaine-des-puys","move":"push","caption":"The Chaîne des Puys, Auvergne: the lava flows of Laschamps recorded the excursion"},{"at":1,"type":"photo","media":"kauri-swamp","move":"pan-right","caption":"Swamp kauri from Northland, New Zealand, dated the event to the decade"},{"at":2,"type":"split","question":"Did the magnetic excursion of 41,000 years ago change human history?","views":[{"label":"A global crisis","text":"Cooper, Turney and colleagues, 2021: ozone loss and climate shifts contributed to extinctions, Neanderthal decline and new behaviours; Mukhopadhyay and colleagues, 2025, add a possible advantage for clothed, ochre-using Homo sapiens.","media":"aurora"},{"label":"Coincidence","text":"Picin, Hawks and others, 2021: the extinctions and the Neanderthal decline began earlier, and ice cores and cave records show no distinct climate event at the time.","media":"chaine-des-puys"}],"status":"Most researchers remain unconvinced of major effects on people.","stagger":6}],"sentences":["About 41,000 years ago Earth’s magnetic field weakened to a fraction of its strength and its poles wandered, an event named after lava flows at Laschamps in France.","In 2021 Alan Cooper, Chris Turney and colleagues dated it precisely with radiocarbon from a kauri tree preserved in a New Zealand swamp, called the crisis the Adams Event, and linked it to megafaunal extinctions in Australia, the end of the Neanderthals and a rise in cave art.","Andrea Picin, John Hawks and others replied that most of those events were already under way thousands of years earlier, and that ice cores show no marked climate change at the time.","In 2025 Agnit Mukhopadhyay and colleagues modelled how auroras and radiation would have reached lower latitudes, and speculated that clothing, ochre and caves gave Homo sapiens an edge over Neanderthals.","The idea is untested."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"The pattern across all three is the same: a striking claim, a search for the evidence that would have to exist if it were true, and a field that moves slowly toward an answer. It is how every argument in this series will be handled.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"type","text":"A striking claim, a search for the evidence that would have to exist if it were true, and a field that moves slowly toward an answer.","kicker":"The pattern"}],"sentences":["The pattern across all three is the same: a striking claim, a search for the evidence that would have to exist if it were true, and a field that moves slowly toward an answer.","It is how every argument in this series will be handled."]}],"sources":[{"cite":"Hu, W. et al. (2023). Genomic inference of a severe human bottleneck during the Early to Middle Pleistocene transition. Science 381, 979–984.","doi":"10.1126/science.abq7487"},{"cite":"Deng, Y., Nielsen, R. & Song, Y. S. (2025). A previously reported bottleneck in human ancestry 900 kya is likely a statistical artifact. Genetics 229, iyae192."},{"cite":"Cousins, T. & Durvasula, A. (2025). Insufficient evidence for a severe bottleneck in humans during the Early to Middle Pleistocene transition. Molecular Biology and Evolution 42, msaf041."},{"cite":"Hu, W. et al. (2026). Severe bottleneck of ancient Homo populations: insights from computational modeling and relevant fossil evidence. Molecular Biology and Evolution 43, msag157."},{"cite":"Ambrose, S. H. (1998). Late Pleistocene human population bottlenecks, volcanic winter, and differentiation of modern humans. Journal of Human Evolution 34, 623–651."},{"cite":"Petraglia, M. et al. (2007). Middle Paleolithic assemblages from the Indian subcontinent before and after the Toba super-eruption. Science 317, 114–116."},{"cite":"Smith, E. I. et al. (2018). Humans thrived in South Africa through the Toba eruption about 74,000 years ago. Nature 555, 511–515."},{"cite":"Kappelman, J. et al. (2024). Adaptive foraging behaviours in the Horn of Africa during Toba supereruption. Nature 628, 365–372."},{"cite":"Cooper, A. et al. (2021). A global environmental crisis 42,000 years ago. Science 371, 811–818.","doi":"10.1126/science.abb8677"},{"cite":"Picin, A. et al. (2021) and Hawks, J. (2021). Comments on “A global environmental crisis 42,000 years ago”. Science 374, eabh1878 and eabh3655."},{"cite":"Mukhopadhyay, A. et al. (2025). Wandering of the auroral oval 41,000 years ago. Science Advances 11, eadq7275."}]},{"id":"the-whole-story-in-one-sweep","title":"The whole story in one sweep","start":7000000,"end":700,"camera":{"lon":25,"lat":5,"zoom":1},"clock":true,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Before the continents get their own episodes, here is the whole story in one sweep. Seven million years ago, in Africa, an ape began to walk upright. Sahelanthropus from Chad, Orrorin from Kenya and Ardipithecus from Ethiopia are the candidates for the earliest members of our lineage, and each is argued over.","sites":["toros-menalla","kapsomin","aramis"],"camera":{"sites":["toros-menalla","kapsomin","aramis"],"zoom":2.6},"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"globe","camera":{"lon":25,"lat":5,"zoom":1}},{"at":1,"type":"photo","media":"rift-valley","move":"push","caption":"The Great Rift Valley, Kenya"},{"at":2,"type":"montage","media":["toumai","orrorin","ardi"],"captions":["Sahelanthropus, Chad, about 7 million years","Orrorin, Kenya, about 6 million years","Ardipithecus, Ethiopia, 4.4 million years"]}],"sentences":["Before the continents get their own episodes, here is the whole story in one sweep.","Seven million years ago, in Africa, an ape began to walk upright.","Sahelanthropus from Chad, Orrorin from Kenya and Ardipithecus from Ethiopia are the candidates for the earliest members of our lineage, and each is argued over."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"By 3.5 million years ago the australopiths, Lucy’s kind, walked the East African rift and the caves of South Africa, and someone at Lomekwi was breaking stone. By 2.8 million years ago the first fossils placed in our own genus, Homo, appear in Ethiopia. By 2 million years ago Homo erectus, tall, long-legged and larger-brained, had appeared; soon it was making handaxes, and hominins had begun to leave Africa.","sites":["hadar","laetoli","sterkfontein","ledi-geraru"],"time":2000000,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"montage","media":["lucy","laetoli-footprints"],"captions":["Lucy, Hadar, 3.2 million years","The Laetoli footprints, 3.66 million years"]},{"at":1,"type":"globe","camera":{"sites":["hadar","laetoli","sterkfontein","ledi-geraru"]}},{"at":2,"type":"photo","media":"turkana-boy","move":"push","caption":"Turkana Boy, Homo erectus, about 1.5 million years"}],"sentences":["By 3.5 million years ago the australopiths, Lucy’s kind, walked the East African rift and the caves of South Africa, and someone at Lomekwi was breaking stone.","By 2.8 million years ago the first fossils placed in our own genus, Homo, appear in Ethiopia.","By 2 million years ago Homo erectus, tall, long-legged and larger-brained, had appeared; soon it was making handaxes, and hominins had begun to leave Africa."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Stone tools 2.1 million years old at Shangchen in China and fossils 1.8 million years old at Dmanisi in Georgia mark that first great expansion. Its descendants reached Java, Flores and Luzon, where island dwarfs evolved, and Europe, where by 400,000 years ago hominins hunted with wooden spears and tended fire.","sites":["shangchen","dmanisi","sangiran","liang-bua"],"camera":{"box":[-10,-10,130,55]},"route":"sweep-erectus","time":1500000,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"globe","camera":{"box":[-10,-10,130,55]}},{"at":1,"type":"montage","media":["dmanisi-skull","floresiensis-skull"],"captions":["A skull from Dmanisi, Georgia, 1.8 million years","Homo floresiensis, the island dwarf of Flores"]}],"sentences":["Stone tools 2.1 million years old at Shangchen in China and fossils 1.8 million years old at Dmanisi in Georgia mark that first great expansion.","Its descendants reached Java, Flores and Luzon, where island dwarfs evolved, and Europe, where by 400,000 years ago hominins hunted with wooden spears and tended fire."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"From about 430,000 years ago the human family branched. Neanderthals held Europe and western Asia; Denisovans, known first from DNA, ranged from Siberia to Tibet and Taiwan; Homo naledi, small-brained and long-armed, left its dead deep in a South African cave, buried deliberately, its discoverers argue. In Africa our own species took shape, with fossils from Jebel Irhoud in Morocco about 315,000 years old, and with beads, ochre and engraved lines appearing over the following 200,000 years.","sites":["sima-de-los-huesos","denisova-cave","rising-star-cave","jebel-irhoud","blombos-cave"],"route":"sweep-erectus-south","time":315000,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"montage","media":["neanderthal-skull","denisova-cave-photo","naledi-skeleton"],"captions":["A Neanderthal skull","Denisova Cave, Siberia","Homo naledi, South Africa"]},{"at":2,"type":"montage","media":["jebel-irhoud-skull","blombos-ochre"],"captions":["Jebel Irhoud, Morocco, about 315,000 years","Engraved ochre from Blombos Cave, South Africa"]}],"sentences":["From about 430,000 years ago the human family branched.","Neanderthals held Europe and western Asia; Denisovans, known first from DNA, ranged from Siberia to Tibet and Taiwan; Homo naledi, small-brained and long-armed, left its dead deep in a South African cave, buried deliberately, its discoverers argue.","In Africa our own species took shape, with fossils from Jebel Irhoud in Morocco about 315,000 years old, and with beads, ochre and engraved lines appearing over the following 200,000 years."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Homo sapiens left Africa more than once. The dispersal that mattered most began about 70,000 years ago, crossed Arabia and southern Asia, and reached Australia by 50,000 years ago, or 65,000 by a contested date, and Europe by 45,000. Neanderthals and Denisovans mixed with the newcomers, and then vanished, leaving their DNA in most people alive today.","sites":["madjedbebe","lake-mungo"],"camera":{"box":[-10,-45,150,60]},"route":"sweep-sapiens","time":45000,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"globe","camera":{"box":[-10,-45,150,60]}},{"at":2,"type":"number","value":"1 to 2 percent","label":"Neanderthal DNA in people outside Africa; Denisovan DNA reaches several percent in New Guinea and Australia","kicker":"Green and colleagues, 2010; Reich and colleagues, 2010"}],"sentences":["Homo sapiens left Africa more than once.","The dispersal that mattered most began about 70,000 years ago, crossed Arabia and southern Asia, and reached Australia by 50,000 years ago, or 65,000 by a contested date, and Europe by 45,000.","Neanderthals and Denisovans mixed with the newcomers, and then vanished, leaving their DNA in most people alive today."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"In the depths of the last ice age people painted caves, carved ivory, domesticated the dog, and crossed from Siberia into the Americas, by 21,000 years ago if the White Sands footprints are as old as their dates say. As the ice melted, people in at least seven regions began to farm, independently: the Levant, China, New Guinea, Mesoamerica, the Andes, eastern North America and the African Sahel.","sites":["white-sands","monte-verde","chauvet-cave"],"camera":{"lon":-60,"lat":20,"zoom":1},"route":"sweep-europe","time":11000,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"montage","media":["chauvet","lion-man","white-sands-footprints"],"captions":["Chauvet Cave, France","The Lion Man of Hohlenstein-Stadel","The White Sands footprints, New Mexico"]},{"at":1,"type":"globe","camera":{"lon":-60,"lat":20,"zoom":1}}],"sentences":["In the depths of the last ice age people painted caves, carved ivory, domesticated the dog, and crossed from Siberia into the Americas, by 21,000 years ago if the White Sands footprints are as old as their dates say.","As the ice melted, people in at least seven regions began to farm, independently: the Levant, China, New Guinea, Mesoamerica, the Andes, eastern North America and the African Sahel."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Villages became towns. Göbekli Tepe’s pillars rose in Anatolia about 11,500 years ago, before pottery and before wheat was fully domesticated. Copper was smelted in the Balkans by 7,000 years ago, and bronze came into everyday use in Mesopotamia about 5,000 years ago. The last voyages of the Stone Age settled the Pacific: Lapita canoes reached Fiji and Tonga about 2,800 years ago, and their descendants reached New Zealand only about 700 years ago, when Europe was building cathedrals.","sites":["gobekli-tepe","belovode","uruk","teouma"],"camera":{"lon":150,"lat":0,"zoom":1},"route":"sweep-americas","time":700,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"catalhoyuk","move":"push","caption":"Çatalhöyük, Anatolia: a town of several thousand people"},{"at":1,"type":"photo","media":"gobekli-pillars","move":"pan-right","caption":"Göbekli Tepe, about 11,500 years ago"},{"at":2,"type":"montage","media":["varna-gold","uruk"],"captions":["Gold from the Varna necropolis, Bulgaria","Uruk, Mesopotamia"]},{"at":3,"type":"montage","media":["lapita-pottery","hokulea","maori-waka"],"captions":["Lapita pottery","A Polynesian voyaging canoe","A Māori waka"]}],"sentences":["Villages became towns.","Göbekli Tepe’s pillars rose in Anatolia about 11,500 years ago, before pottery and before wheat was fully domesticated.","Copper was smelted in the Balkans by 7,000 years ago, and bronze came into everyday use in Mesopotamia about 5,000 years ago.","The last voyages of the Stone Age settled the Pacific: Lapita canoes reached Fiji and Tonga about 2,800 years ago, and their descendants reached New Zealand only about 700 years ago, when Europe was building cathedrals."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"That is the sweep. Each episode from here will slow it down, continent by continent, and let the arguments breathe.","route":"sweep-pacific","camera":{"lon":20,"lat":10,"zoom":1},"card":{"type":"text","kicker":"The series","title":"Eight episodes","items":["1. The Longest Age","2. Africa: the Cradle","3. Africa: Our Species","4. Europe","5. West and South Asia","6. East and North Asia","7. The Americas","8. Sahul and the Pacific"]},"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"globe","camera":{"lon":20,"lat":10,"zoom":1}}],"sentences":["That is the sweep.","Each episode from here will slow it down, continent by continent, and let the arguments breathe."]}],"sources":[{"cite":"Wood, B. & Boyle, E. K. (2016). Hominin taxic diversity: fact or fantasy? Yearbook of Physical Anthropology 159, S37–S78."},{"cite":"Hublin, J.-J. et al. (2017). New fossils from Jebel Irhoud, Morocco and the pan-African origin of Homo sapiens. Nature 546, 289–292."},{"cite":"Zhu, Z. et al. (2018). Hominin occupation of the Chinese Loess Plateau since about 2.1 million years ago. Nature 559, 608–612."},{"cite":"Bennett, M. R. et al. (2021). Evidence of humans in North America during the Last Glacial Maximum. Science 373, 1528–1531."},{"cite":"Larson, G. et al. (2014). Current perspectives and the future of domestication studies. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 111, 6139–6146."}]},{"id":"who-counts-as-human","title":"Who counts as human","start":60000,"end":40000,"camera":{"sites":["la-chapelle-aux-saints"],"zoom":4.5},"clock":true,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","text":"A word this series uses constantly needs a definition. A hominin is a human or any extinct relative more closely related to us than to chimpanzees and bonobos. The word says nothing about brains, tools or speech; it is about a branch of the family tree.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"chimp-bonobo","move":"push","caption":"Chimpanzees and bonobos, our closest living relatives"},{"at":2,"type":"photo","media":"hominin-tree","move":"pull","caption":"The hominin family tree: a branch, not a ladder","fit":"contain"}],"sentences":["A word this series uses constantly needs a definition.","A hominin is a human or any extinct relative more closely related to us than to chimpanzees and bonobos.","The word says nothing about brains, tools or speech; it is about a branch of the family tree."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Species names are working hypotheses. Two researchers looking at the same jaw may place it in different species, and the record is thin: the whole story of the first three million years rests on a few thousand fragments. This series will name arguments as arguments and say who holds each view. Where genetics has changed the picture, as it has for the Neanderthals, the Denisovans and our own origins, it will say how.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"dmanisi-skull","move":"pan-right","caption":"One skull, several opinions: Dmanisi"},{"at":2,"type":"type","text":"This series will name arguments as arguments, and say who holds each view.","kicker":"How to read the series"}],"sentences":["Species names are working hypotheses.","Two researchers looking at the same jaw may place it in different species, and the record is thin: the whole story of the first three million years rests on a few thousand fragments.","This series will name arguments as arguments and say who holds each view.","Where genetics has changed the picture, as it has for the Neanderthals, the Denisovans and our own origins, it will say how."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"The hunched, grunting caveman of cartoons is a nineteenth-century invention. Lubbock, who coined Palaeolithic, explained ancient Europeans through comparison with what he called modern savages. Between 1911 and 1913 Marcellin Boule reconstructed the Neanderthal skeleton from La Chapelle-aux-Saints in France as a stooped, bent-kneed brute. In 1957 William Straus and Alexander Cave showed that the man had severe arthritis, and that Neanderthals stood as upright as we do.","sites":["la-chapelle-aux-saints"],"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"neanderthal-reconstruction","move":"push","caption":"A modern reconstruction of a Neanderthal"},{"at":1,"type":"photo","media":"prehistoric-times-1","move":"pan-left","caption":"Lubbock’s Pre-historic Times, 1865","fit":"contain"},{"at":2,"type":"photo","media":"boule-1911","move":"pull","caption":"Boule’s reconstruction of the La Chapelle-aux-Saints Neanderthal, 1911 to 1913","lower":{"name":"Marcellin Boule","sub":"Muséum national d’Histoire naturelle, Paris"}},{"at":3,"type":"photo","media":"la-chapelle-skeleton","move":"tilt-up","caption":"The La Chapelle-aux-Saints skeleton: an old man with severe arthritis"}],"sentences":["The hunched, grunting caveman of cartoons is a nineteenth-century invention.","Lubbock, who coined Palaeolithic, explained ancient Europeans through comparison with what he called modern savages.","Between 1911 and 1913 Marcellin Boule reconstructed the Neanderthal skeleton from La Chapelle-aux-Saints in France as a stooped, bent-kneed brute.","In 1957 William Straus and Alexander Cave showed that the man had severe arthritis, and that Neanderthals stood as upright as we do."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"The same thinking justified colonial rule. Aboriginal Australians, Tasmanians, the San and others were labelled Stone Age peoples or living fossils, as if they had no history. In fact, living foragers such as the Hadza of Tanzania and the San of the Kalahari have long histories of change, trade and contact with farmers and states, as the Kalahari debate opened by Edwin Wilmsen in 1989 made clear. They are contemporary peoples. The Stone Age is a label for tools, not for the worth of the people who used them.","camera":{"box":[10,-35,45,0]},"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"globe","camera":{"box":[10,-35,45,0]}},{"at":2,"type":"montage","media":["hadza","san-people"],"captions":["Hadza people, Tanzania","San people, Kalahari"]},{"at":4,"type":"type","text":"The Stone Age is a label for tools, not for the worth of the people who used them."}],"sentences":["The same thinking justified colonial rule.","Aboriginal Australians, Tasmanians, the San and others were labelled Stone Age peoples or living fossils, as if they had no history.","In fact, living foragers such as the Hadza of Tanzania and the San of the Kalahari have long histories of change, trade and contact with farmers and states, as the Kalahari debate opened by Edwin Wilmsen in 1989 made clear.","They are contemporary peoples.","The Stone Age is a label for tools, not for the worth of the people who used them."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"The people of the Stone Age crossed seas, painted caves, made music, cared for the sick and injured, and shaped whole landscapes with fire. Their descendants include everyone alive today.","shots":[{"at":0,"type":"montage","media":["lascaux","chauvet"],"captions":["Lascaux","Chauvet"]},{"at":1,"type":"type","text":"Their descendants include everyone alive today."}],"sentences":["The people of the Stone Age crossed seas, painted caves, made music, cared for the sick and injured, and shaped whole landscapes with fire.","Their descendants include everyone alive today."]}],"sources":[{"cite":"Boule, M. (1911–1913). L’homme fossile de La Chapelle-aux-Saints. Annales de Paléontologie 6–8."},{"cite":"Straus, W. L. & Cave, A. J. E. (1957). Pathology and the posture of Neanderthal man. Quarterly Review of Biology 32, 348–363."},{"cite":"Wilmsen, E. N. (1989). Land Filled with Flies: A Political Economy of the Kalahari. University of Chicago Press."},{"cite":"Wood, B. & Harrison, T. (2011). The evolutionary context of the first hominins. Nature 470, 347–352."}]},{"id":"no-single-end","title":"No single end","start":7000,"end":2500,"camera":{"lon":40,"lat":25,"zoom":1},"clock":true,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","text":"The Stone Age has no single end. In this series it ends, region by region, when metal came into everyday use. Southwest Asia and southeast Europe went first: smelted copper by about 7,000 years ago at Belovode in Serbia, bronze common in Mesopotamia by about 5,000. China used bronze on a large scale from about 4,000 years ago. Much of sub-Saharan Africa went from stone straight to iron within the last 3,000 years, with no Bronze Age at all. In the Americas, people hammered native copper around the Great Lakes for thousands of years and Andean societies later smelted gold, silver and copper, but stone remained the everyday material. Australia and most Pacific islands used no metal before European contact.","sites":["belovode","uruk","erlitou"],"time":3000,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"globe","camera":{"lon":40,"lat":25,"zoom":1}},{"at":2,"type":"montage","media":["belovode-photo","varna-gold"],"captions":["Belovode, Serbia: copper smelting about 7,000 years ago","Varna, Bulgaria: the oldest gold hoard"]},{"at":3,"type":"photo","media":"erlitou-bronze","move":"push","caption":"A bronze vessel from Erlitou, China"},{"at":4,"type":"globe","camera":{"box":[-20,-35,55,20]}},{"at":5,"type":"montage","media":["old-copper","chavin-gold"],"captions":["Hammered native copper from the Great Lakes","Gold from Chavín, Peru"]},{"at":6,"type":"photo","media":"aboriginal-stone-tools","move":"pan-right","caption":"Stone tools from Australia, used until European contact"}],"sentences":["The Stone Age has no single end.","In this series it ends, region by region, when metal came into everyday use.","Southwest Asia and southeast Europe went first: smelted copper by about 7,000 years ago at Belovode in Serbia, bronze common in Mesopotamia by about 5,000.","China used bronze on a large scale from about 4,000 years ago.","Much of sub-Saharan Africa went from stone straight to iron within the last 3,000 years, with no Bronze Age at all.","In the Americas, people hammered native copper around the Great Lakes for thousands of years and Andean societies later smelted gold, silver and copper, but stone remained the everyday material.","Australia and most Pacific islands used no metal before European contact."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Stone stayed useful long after. Flint sickles remained common in the Bronze Age. In highland New Guinea, ground stone axes were quarried and traded into the twentieth century. An obsidian blade has an edge finer than a surgical scalpel, and some surgeons have used them. When this series says the Stone Age ended, it means only that a new material had arrived. The skills did not leave.","camera":{"sites":["kuk-swamp"],"zoom":2.5},"time":2500,"shots":[{"at":1,"type":"photo","media":"flint-sickle","move":"push","caption":"A flint sickle, still in use in the Bronze Age"},{"at":2,"type":"photo","media":"new-guinea-axe","move":"pan-left","caption":"A ground stone axe from highland New Guinea"},{"at":3,"type":"photo","media":"obsidian-blade","move":"tilt-up","caption":"An obsidian blade: an edge finer than a scalpel"},{"at":4,"type":"type","text":"The skills did not leave."}],"sentences":["Stone stayed useful long after.","Flint sickles remained common in the Bronze Age.","In highland New Guinea, ground stone axes were quarried and traded into the twentieth century.","An obsidian blade has an edge finer than a surgical scalpel, and some surgeons have used them.","When this series says the Stone Age ended, it means only that a new material had arrived.","The skills did not leave."]}],"sources":[{"cite":"Radivojević, M. et al. (2010). On the origins of extractive metallurgy: new evidence from Europe. Journal of Archaeological Science 37, 2775–2787."},{"cite":"Killick, D. (2009). Cairo to Cape: the spread of metallurgy through eastern and southern Africa. Journal of World Prehistory 22, 399–414."},{"cite":"Shea, J. J. (2013). Lithic modes A–I: a new framework for describing global-scale variation in stone tool technology illustrated with evidence from the East Mediterranean Levant. Journal of Archaeological Method and Theory 20, 151–186."}]},{"id":"the-questions-ahead","title":"The questions ahead","start":65000,"end":11500,"camera":{"lon":-100,"lat":20,"zoom":1},"clock":true,"cues":[{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Big questions remain open, and this series will follow them. When did people first reach the Americas: 23,000 years ago, as the White Sands footprints suggest, with three kinds of radiocarbon dates now agreeing, or after 16,000, as most sites indicate? When did they reach Australia: 65,000 years ago, if the sand at Madjedbebe holds its artefacts in place, or about 50,000, as genetics and most other sites suggest? Who made the first tools, and was Lomekwi one of their sites? What did the Denisovans look like, now that one skull has a name? Did Homo naledi bury its dead? Why did our species alone survive? Did farming begin in one place and spread, or in many? And how did inequality begin?","sites":["white-sands","madjedbebe"],"camera":{"sites":["white-sands","madjedbebe"]},"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"white-sands-footprints","move":"push","caption":"The White Sands footprints, New Mexico"},{"at":1,"type":"photo","media":"madjedbebe","move":"pan-right","caption":"Madjedbebe rock shelter, Arnhem Land"},{"at":2,"type":"globe","camera":{"sites":["lomekwi-3"],"zoom":3}},{"at":3,"type":"photo","media":"denisovan-reconstruction","move":"pull","caption":"A reconstruction of a Denisovan"},{"at":4,"type":"photo","media":"dinaledi","move":"tilt-up","caption":"The Dinaledi chamber, Rising Star Cave"},{"at":5,"type":"globe","camera":{"lon":20,"lat":10,"zoom":1}}],"sentences":["Big questions remain open, and this series will follow them.","When did people first reach the Americas: 23,000 years ago, as the White Sands footprints suggest, with three kinds of radiocarbon dates now agreeing, or after 16,000, as most sites indicate?","When did they reach Australia: 65,000 years ago, if the sand at Madjedbebe holds its artefacts in place, or about 50,000, as genetics and most other sites suggest?","Who made the first tools, and was Lomekwi one of their sites?","What did the Denisovans look like, now that one skull has a name?","Did Homo naledi bury its dead?","Why did our species alone survive?","Did farming begin in one place and spread, or in many?","And how did inequality begin?"]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Alongside these, the series will weigh views that most specialists reject but that have published proponents and a large public: the comet that ended the ice age; Göbekli Tepe as a record of the stars; a lost civilisation of the ice age; a pyramid older than the pyramids at Gunung Padang in Java; a Stone Age crossing of the Atlantic. Each will get the same treatment: the claim as its supporters put it, the evidence they cite, the answers of critics, and where the field stands, so that you can weigh it yourself.","camera":{"sites":["gobekli-tepe"],"zoom":1.6},"time":11500,"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"photo","media":"gobekli-pillars","move":"push","caption":"Göbekli Tepe"},{"at":3,"type":"photo","media":"gunung-padang","move":"pan-left","caption":"Gunung Padang, Java"},{"at":4,"type":"photo","media":"solutrean-point","move":"pull","caption":"A Solutrean point, France, about 20,000 years old"}],"sentences":["Alongside these, the series will weigh views that most specialists reject but that have published proponents and a large public: the comet that ended the ice age; Göbekli Tepe as a record of the stars; a lost civilisation of the ice age; a pyramid older than the pyramids at Gunung Padang in Java; a Stone Age crossing of the Atlantic.","Each will get the same treatment: the claim as its supporters put it, the evidence they cite, the answers of critics, and where the field stands, so that you can weigh it yourself."]},{"speaker":"narrator","text":"Next time, the story begins where the fossils are thickest. Episode 2, Africa, the cradle: from the first upright apes to the first fires, and to a small-brained human who may have buried its dead.","camera":{"lon":25,"lat":5,"zoom":1.3},"shots":[{"at":0,"type":"title","kicker":"Next","title":"Africa: the Cradle","sub":"Episode 2"},{"at":1,"type":"credits","kicker":"Pictures in this episode"}],"sentences":["Next time, the story begins where the fossils are thickest.","Episode 2, Africa, the cradle: from the first upright apes to the first fires, and to a small-brained human who may have buried its dead."]}],"sources":[{"cite":"Pigati, J. S. et al. (2023). Independent age estimates resolve the controversy of ancient human footprints at White Sands. Science 382, 73–75.","doi":"10.1126/science.adh5007"},{"cite":"Holliday, V. T. et al. (2025). Paleolake geochronology supports Late Pleistocene (21,000 to 23,000 years) age for human footprints at White Sands. Science Advances 11."},{"cite":"Clarkson, C. et al. (2017). Human occupation of northern Australia by 65,000 years ago. Nature 547, 306–310.","doi":"10.1038/nature22968"},{"cite":"Allen, J. & O’Connell, J. F. (2020). A different paradigm for the initial colonisation of Sahul. Archaeology in Oceania 55, 1–14."},{"cite":"Berger, L. R. et al. (2025). Evidence for deliberate burial of the dead by Homo naledi. eLife 12, RP89106."}]}]}]};
